WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:17.820
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, this&nbsp;
is Tim Ferriss. Welcome to another episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.200
of The Tim Ferriss Show, where it is my job to&nbsp;
deconstruct, or at least attempt to deconstruct,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.740
interview, shall we say, world-class&nbsp;
performers from all different disciplines.&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.260
My guest today, I feel for me, has been a&nbsp;
long time coming. I'm very excited to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:36.780
on the podcast Dr. Gül Dölen. Dr. Gül Dölen is&nbsp;
an associate professor of neuroscience at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.460
Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine, and a&nbsp;
pioneer and world leader of psychedelics research.&nbsp;

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.840
Her laboratory has discovered a novel mechanism&nbsp;
that could account for the broad range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.580
therapeutic applications that psychedelics are&nbsp;
currently being tested for. Her lab has discovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.900
a novel critical period, we'll define what&nbsp;
these things mean, for social reward learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.700
and shown that this critical can be reopened&nbsp;
with psychedelic drugs such as MDMA, LSD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.020
psilocybin, ketamine, and ibogaine.
Building on this discovery, she has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:09.000
formulated the hypothesis that psychedelics may be&nbsp;
the long-sought master key for unlocking critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.740
periods across the brain. To test this hypothesis,&nbsp;
she's initiated a nationwide collaborative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.180
effort to determine whether psychedelics reopen&nbsp;
critical periods for ocular dominance plasticity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:24.120
birdsong learning, anatomical plasticity in the&nbsp;
barrel cortex, serotonergic neuronal regeneration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:29.580
dendritic spinogenesis, and motor learning. If&nbsp;
you don't know what those words or phrases mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.900
don't worry, we will define them as we go.
Importantly, understanding psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.400
through this framework dramatically expands the&nbsp;
scope of disorders, including autism, stroke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.080
and allergies that might benefit from adjunct&nbsp;
therapy with psychedelics, an approach she has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:48.900
dubbed the PHATHOM project. That's P-H-A-T-H-O-M,&nbsp;
which stands for Psychedelic Healing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:55.020
Adjunct Therapy Harnessing Opened Malleability.
Dr. Dölen earned her MD PhD at Brown University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:59.640
and the Massachusetts Institute of Technology,&nbsp;
and otherwise known as MIT, where she carried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.080
out seminal work on critical periods, learning&nbsp;
and memory, and the pathogenesis of autism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:12.283
You can find her online at dolenlab.org. Dr.&nbsp;
Dölen, welcome to the show. Nice to have you.&nbsp;

00:02:12.283 --> 00:02:14.700
Dr. Gül Dölen: Thank you for&nbsp;
having me. It's nice to be here.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.440
Tim Ferriss: So I thought we might&nbsp;
start with your undergrad experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:26.040
and I have read that you designed your own major,&nbsp;
and I was hoping you could describe how one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:31.980
designs one's own major and how you thought&nbsp;
about going about designing your own major.&nbsp;

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:40.140
Dr. Gül Dölen: I've always been somebody who&nbsp;
learns best by comparing. And originally I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:47.340
thought, well, I'll just do a triple major of&nbsp;
neuroscience and linguistics and philosophy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:52.200
And the university was like, "No, no, there's&nbsp;
no triple major. You can do a double major,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:59.280
but if you really need all three, we recommend&nbsp;
that you do this, design your own major path."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:07.500
And so I worked with my professors in philosophy&nbsp;
in neuroscience primarily to come up with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.120
curriculum that would span all of the different&nbsp;
elements that I wanted to incorporate in trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:21.960
to ask the question, "What is the mind? What&nbsp;
is consciousness? How do we know that from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:26.700
different perspectives?" And so the major was&nbsp;
called Comparative Perspectives on the Mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:33.180
and it was a combination of neuroscience,&nbsp;
philosophy, linguistics, art, religion.&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.740
And really the idea was is that this is something&nbsp;
that humans care about. And so we've thought about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:46.620
it from all of these different perspectives&nbsp;
for millennia and what can we understand from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:51.540
that approach?
Tim Ferriss:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:59.160
Why were those questions — and hopefully this&nbsp;
doesn't sound like a stupid question, because at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:03.840
face value perhaps they should be interesting&nbsp;
questions to everyone since, on some level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.400
I suppose consciousness is fundamental —&nbsp;
but why were these questions interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.840
compelling to you at the time enough that&nbsp;
you would attempt to have a triple major?&nbsp;

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:21.420
Dr. Gül Dölen: I have to say I'm not really sure&nbsp;
why exactly those, compared to everything else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:30.300
that I saw other people majoring in like economics&nbsp;
or history, I just didn't care about those things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:41.640
the same way as I cared about. I've always wanted&nbsp;
to only study or think about things that I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:46.800
are deeply interesting that I would want to talk&nbsp;
to with anybody. Whether it be the person sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:54.180
next to me on the bus or my parents or somebody's&nbsp;
kid. I like thinking about things and when I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:59.280
obsessed with something, I just think about it all&nbsp;
the time in every context. And so I just never had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:06.600
that with economics or history or any of the&nbsp;
other topics that seem to be popular majors.&nbsp;

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.611
Tim Ferriss: Didn't cause the quickening.
Dr. Gül Dölen: Right.&nbsp;

00:05:10.611 --> 00:05:17.220
Tim Ferriss: There are a few terms that&nbsp;
may come up later, we'll see. And I'd love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:24.960
to have you just expand on them or define them&nbsp;
because I was reading in prep for this and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.180
briefly considered marine biology, but you were&nbsp;
also interested in philosophy of mind and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:38.280
a philosophical consideration called theory of&nbsp;
mind. What is philosophy of mind? How should we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:42.360
think about it? What is theory of mind?
Dr. Gül Dölen: Philosophy of mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:47.640
it's not really a branch of philosophy&nbsp;
or anything, it's just a thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.880
has drawn the attention of philosophers from&nbsp;
a variety of different perspectives like you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:59.280
can imagine considering what is the mind from a&nbsp;
metaphysical point of view? So what are the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:06.060
that we count when we say this is what a mind&nbsp;
is, this is what a mind is not. Then there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:12.120
other considerations like, "Well, how do we know&nbsp;
mind? Can we know mind just through introspection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:18.720
or is there some empirical way or some way that&nbsp;
we can compare different people's descriptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:24.360
of those things to get at what is mind?" And&nbsp;
that would be the more epistemological approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:29.940
to mind. And I was interested in all of those&nbsp;
things. There continues to be debate about that.&nbsp;

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:38.640
And one of the interesting ideas that I came&nbsp;
across was this, that there was this concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:44.400
of theory of mind, which is not really a&nbsp;
theory in the way that we have a theory of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:53.820
light or a theory of synaptogenesis or any of the&nbsp;
more commonplace theories. It's more like it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:57.120
point of view. The theory of mind is just — the&nbsp;
shorthand way of explaining what theory of mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:03.360
that I like to tell people is, it's sort of what&nbsp;
you're doing. It's the neurobiological process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:09.120
that is going on when you're playing poker.&nbsp;
It's anticipating what somebody else might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:14.220
thinking and trying to see the world from their&nbsp;
point of view. So in some sense it's a theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:19.320
about what somebody else might be thinking.
And this ability to understand or to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:25.620
concept of what somebody else might be thinking&nbsp;
is something that we develop and we get better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:32.160
at as we mature. You can always tell when a kid&nbsp;
is starting to pick up theory of mind, it first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:37.920
comes on around age four or five because they&nbsp;
start doing these funny pranking behaviors where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:44.880
they'll reach around your back and tap you on the&nbsp;
wrong side and look the other way. And that's them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:49.800
sort of playing with the idea that their own&nbsp;
point of view and the person whose shoulder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:54.900
they're tapping might be different and that they&nbsp;
can trick somebody by playing on that difference.&nbsp;

00:07:54.900 --> 00:08:04.260
And so this theory of mind, there was some&nbsp;
idea out there, I'm not sure that it's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:09.900
a dominant theory right now in autism, but there&nbsp;
was an idea that this is something that people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:16.200
with autism are not so good at and it makes them&nbsp;
bad poker players, it makes them bad at lying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:22.440
it makes them bad at tricking people. And&nbsp;
it's this thing that psychopaths, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:28.320
are very good at. They have better-than-average&nbsp;
theory of mind, and they use that better than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:33.420
average theory of mind to manipulate people&nbsp;
by anticipating what they may or may not like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:38.640
and using that information to try and get&nbsp;
whatever it is that they're trying to get.&nbsp;

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:48.120
And so this theory of mind idea was out&nbsp;
there and there was some idea that, "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:53.220
you need to have theory of mind in order to have&nbsp;
consciousness, in order to have a full sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.120 --> 00:09:01.140
knowing who you are compared to other people."&nbsp;
And I like this idea because it was a testable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:07.620
hypothesis. And one of the things that I found&nbsp;
frustrating about being a philosopher is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:12.840
the philosophers were always asking the&nbsp;
coolest questions, the biggest questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:18.360
but just felt like they were just going around&nbsp;
in circles and never really coming up with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:26.040
answer that I found to be deeply satisfying. And&nbsp;
on the other hand, scientists, neuroscientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:30.960
maybe they weren't getting to ask all the&nbsp;
really big questions, but man, they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:35.940
coming up with some concrete answers that felt&nbsp;
like tangible and like we were getting somewhere.&nbsp;

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:43.380
And so I like this idea that we could test the&nbsp;
requirement of theory of mind for consciousness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:51.120
by looking at diseases where that function&nbsp;
is impaired and learn something about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:56.040
relationship between consciousness and&nbsp;
genes that encode different proteins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.935
that are required for that theory of mind.
Tim Ferriss: I have many, many follow-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.935 --> 00:10:04.500
questions. Theory of mind does not seem&nbsp;
to be — based on the reading that I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:10.380
done — entirely human-specific. And is it true&nbsp;
that you have observed this in, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:16.131
and your tapping on the shoulder made me&nbsp;
think of it, hunting behavior in octopuses?&nbsp;

00:10:16.131 --> 00:10:22.920
Dr. Gül Dölen: Those were actually not my&nbsp;
experiments. Those were experiments done by really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:30.840
imminent octopus researcher Roy Caldwell. And&nbsp;
he showed me the videos of them and as soon as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:38.760
I saw the videos of the octopuses hunting,&nbsp;
it occurred to me that is exactly a theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:44.760
of mind-like behavior in an octopus. And the&nbsp;
reason that that was so interesting is because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:53.760
the way that people talk about theory of mind is&nbsp;
that this is a behavior that evolved due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.760 --> 00:11:01.920
selection pressures imposed by social living. So&nbsp;
when we have to live in a social group, it serves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:08.280
us to be able to anticipate what other members of&nbsp;
that social group might be thinking in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:15.240
anticipate and either compete or collaborate&nbsp;
or whatever it is that you're trying to do.&nbsp;

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:24.300
And octopuses, by and large, are not social&nbsp;
species. So if you put two octopuses of the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:30.540
species into a tank together, they will kill each&nbsp;
other. And there are differences in the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:37.140
aggressiveness that we observe in octopuses. Some&nbsp;
are so aggressive that we've never even seen the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:43.920
males of the species because basically when&nbsp;
they mate, they leave their hectocotylus arm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:50.580
which is their reproductive arm in the mantle&nbsp;
of the female and eject it and escape so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:54.660
they don't get eaten. So we've only ever found&nbsp;
their hectocotylus arm. That's how aggressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.660 --> 00:12:01.980
that species is. Other species of octopus aren't&nbsp;
quite so aggressive, but they're still asocial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:09.660
And it was interesting because the species of&nbsp;
octopus that does that hunting behavior is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:16.260
only known social species of octopus.
And so it seems like an interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:22.500
opportunity to test these ideas about what are&nbsp;
the selection pressures that make theory of mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:32.280
develop across evolution and what are the&nbsp;
species specific advantages to having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:38.280
theory of mind? But it's interesting also because&nbsp;
even though the larger Pacific stripe octopus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:43.740
which is the octopus that does that hunting&nbsp;
behavior is social, it's not using this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:49.560
behavior in a social context, it's using it in a&nbsp;
hunting context. Which suggests that maybe theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:55.980
of mind originated from a hunting behavior&nbsp;
where you can imagine it would be useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:03.480
for the animal to be able to anticipate where&nbsp;
its food might hide, if it was being chased,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:08.880
because octopuses eat shrimp and&nbsp;
fish and they're excellent hunters.&nbsp;

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:12.960
And so the idea is that, well,&nbsp;
maybe these ideas that social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:20.760
behaviors are what drive the evolution of theory&nbsp;
of mind is not quite right and that it emerged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:28.380
by something earlier like hunting behavior.
Tim Ferriss: It strikes me, as a hunter myself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:32.940
which really only was adult onset hunting in&nbsp;
my thirties when I was writing about food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:39.840
I felt sort of obligated to explore these things,&nbsp;
that you would observe behaviors that you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:48.600
map to theory of mind in thousands of species that&nbsp;
are really effective hunters. And I would love to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:58.560
bring up three words that are in your intro, which&nbsp;
I had read earlier that people may not associate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:04.500
with the conversation around psychedelics&nbsp;
currently: autism, stroke, and allergies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:11.880
Where did your interest in autism begin?
Dr. Gül Dölen: For me, autism was something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:19.500
I became interested in when I was an undergrad&nbsp;
thinking about these problems of theory of mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:27.300
and consciousness because I was intrigued by&nbsp;
the possibility that autism could be thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:35.160
of as an impairment in theory of mind, and that it&nbsp;
would be sort of a case example of what happens to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:43.980
your ability to have a fully conscious experience&nbsp;
in the absence of or under conditions of impaired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:51.540
theory of mind. And so how that relates to&nbsp;
psychedelics is really a long-ish winding story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.380 --> 00:15:01.380
which is that when I was a graduate student,&nbsp;
my PhD work was focused on trying to find a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:07.380
therapy for autism. And we had focused in on&nbsp;
one specific type of autism called Fragile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:14.460
X. It's the first identified cause of autism.&nbsp;
It's currently the most abundant or the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:20.760
common cause of autism is a mutation in&nbsp;
the FMR1 gene, which encodes Fragile X.&nbsp;

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:30.240
And what we had found is that we could do a&nbsp;
biochemical manipulation to correct a cellular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:38.040
imbalance that in mice corrected many of the&nbsp;
symptoms of autism that we could measure at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:45.960
time. And so that was really exciting. Something&nbsp;
like 26 other labs followed up on that work and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:52.320
several big pharmaceutical companies said, "Okay,&nbsp;
this is it. This is the mechanism based therapy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.320 --> 00:16:01.380
that we've been looking for." And ran several&nbsp;
very large-scale human clinical trials for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:08.580
metabotropic glutamate receptor modulator that we&nbsp;
thought would correct the imbalance in Fragile X.&nbsp;

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:15.480
And when they did those studies, everybody was&nbsp;
hugely disappointed because we were expecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:20.940
the drugs to work and to cure the disease. And&nbsp;
we really got very little evidence to support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:27.780
that view. And I think at the time all the people&nbsp;
who were doing human research and clinical trials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:33.480
were just willing to throw their hands up and say,&nbsp;
"Well, see, we told you a mouse is not a human.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:39.120
And that's why the trials didn't work because all&nbsp;
of the human trials were based on mouse studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:45.360
and this was just where we went wrong." But a&nbsp;
few of us took a more circumspect view and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:49.620
"Well, yeah, okay, mice aren't humans.&nbsp;
But there were a lot of other differences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:56.520
between the human trials and the mouse trials."
And to me, the one that really stands out is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.520 --> 00:17:04.080
while all of the mouse studies that were done,&nbsp;
the clinical intervention, the alteration of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:13.260
the receptor were given either right at birth or&nbsp;
at genesis or very early in development. Whereas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:19.980
all of the human trials, the drugs were given&nbsp;
to adults. And it occurred to me that because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:27.840
of the timing of when the therapies were given,&nbsp;
all of the mouse studies would've been trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:33.900
correct the underlying biochemical imbalance when&nbsp;
the animal's critical periods were all still open.&nbsp;

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:39.480
Whereas in the human trials, the relevant&nbsp;
critical periods would've been closed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:45.780
time we gave the intervention. And so the&nbsp;
way that I'm imagining that a psychedelic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:53.760
might be useful in the case of autism is not&nbsp;
to cure the primary underlying problems with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.760 --> 00:18:00.600
social behaviors directly with psychedelics, but&nbsp;
rather to pair the psychedelic intervention with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:07.200
the intervention that we think is restoring&nbsp;
the biochemical balance. And when we do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:14.220
we feel like we can restore the biochemical&nbsp;
imbalance while reopening the relevant critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.220 --> 00:18:20.520
period so that the patient can learn from their&nbsp;
social environment in the normal way under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:28.320
conditions of the restored biochemical imbalance.
Tim Ferriss: So I want to eventually ask you what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:34.740
reopening the critical period looks like,&nbsp;
chemically or anatomically. But before we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:39.240
get to that, I would love for you to help me&nbsp;
separate fact from fiction with autism. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:47.100
not a condition that I know much about and&nbsp;
I am nonetheless exposed to many opinions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:54.240
about autism. And people say for instance, that&nbsp;
the prevalence is skyrocketing, that it may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:59.760
caused by people having children when they're&nbsp;
older or it's caused by God knows what, BPA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:06.840
you name it. There's probably a million different&nbsp;
theories out there. And I would be curious to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:17.820
as a scientist, what appears to be most plausible&nbsp;
as causes of autism? Are rates increasing or are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:23.520
we just getting better at diagnosing or looking&nbsp;
for that diagnosis, et cetera? Could you just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:30.240
help give us the ABCs of fact versus fiction?
Dr. Gül Dölen: This is a great question and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:33.900
one that I'm only going to be able to&nbsp;
give you a partial answer for because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:43.440
things are changing rapidly around this. So when&nbsp;
I first started studying autism 20 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:50.340
it was very narrowly defined as a disease.&nbsp;
There was only five or six different genes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:58.320
that had been implicated in causing autism.&nbsp;
It was incredibly highly inherited. So if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:04.620
can give a heritability score for different&nbsp;
diseases and different inheritance patterns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:10.500
hair color and eye color might give you a&nbsp;
heritability score of one. Whereas something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:19.020
like cervical cancer, which is caused by a virus,&nbsp;
has a heritability score of closer to zero. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:26.040
autism had a heritability score of something&nbsp;
like 0.96. So very, very highly heritable.&nbsp;

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:32.880
And it made it different from almost all of the&nbsp;
other neuropsychiatric diseases out there because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:42.060
things like depression and anxiety are hovering&nbsp;
more around the 0.5 range. So sometimes inherited,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:48.060
sometimes environmental, usually a&nbsp;
combination of those factors. And at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:55.260
the time that diagnosis was so narrowly defined&nbsp;
that you got this high heritability. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.220 --> 00:21:01.620
for a variety of different reasons, some of&nbsp;
them scientific, some of them socioeconomic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:10.080
the definition of autism got expanded. In&nbsp;
the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:17.820
Neuropsychiatric Disease, the expanded definition&nbsp;
started including the spectrum of autism.&nbsp;

00:21:18.600 --> 00:21:25.020
Now rather than having — you absolutely have to&nbsp;
have an impairment in social behaviors and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:32.760
impairment in locomotor, stereotypies and all&nbsp;
of these other developmental criteria. It got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:37.620
expanded and a lot more things started&nbsp;
being included. And when this happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:47.040
the heritability estimates of the disease&nbsp;
dropped. And so my interpretation of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:56.760
data is that basically we stopped defining&nbsp;
a disease that was a unified single disease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.760 --> 00:22:02.580
and started including a lot more things that were&nbsp;
not what I would consider to be classical autism.&nbsp;

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:11.040
Now it feels like to me that this has gotten so&nbsp;
expansive that were essentially including things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.040 --> 00:22:20.760
like ADHD with social anxiety or all kinds of&nbsp;
other things that while they might be in the realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:28.500
of autism, it makes it much harder to identify it&nbsp;
and say, "This is what's being caused." And as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:34.080
scientist, I find this to be something that makes&nbsp;
my life much more difficult if we are no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:40.860
defining the disease in a very narrow way. So&nbsp;
when this happens, it's a little bit frustrating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:49.920
And so I have tended to focus on those early&nbsp;
identified genes like FMR1, which have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:56.340
known to be a cause of autism for a long time.&nbsp;
And more importantly, when we look at all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.340 --> 00:23:01.140
other genes that have been implicated in autism,&nbsp;
so now we're somewhere in the approximately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:08.340
a thousand genes have been implicated in autism.&nbsp;
FMR1 seems to be a node. So in other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:16.320
the protein that FMR1 encodes regulates roughly&nbsp;
25 percent of all the other autism genes.&nbsp;

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:23.100
And so to me, that's a good place to start to try&nbsp;
and understand autism. And then I will leave it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:29.880
up to the epidemiologists to sort out how to&nbsp;
redefine the category so that we're capturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:37.200
the right subset of patients and excluding&nbsp;
the subset that are not going to respond to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:42.720
therapy if we're targeting the subset&nbsp;
that are defined in one specific way.&nbsp;

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:51.120
Tim Ferriss: If we look at FMR1 just as a node&nbsp;
in a focal point, and I apologize that I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:55.320
know the answer to this in advance, but does&nbsp;
that mean that in the same way that if, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.320 --> 00:24:03.360
a couple were planning on having children or&nbsp;
someone were looking at say, surrogacy, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:08.700
could do genetic screening? That it's possible for&nbsp;
people to currently do genetic screening for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:15.900
probability of autism as an outcome in offspring?
Dr. Gül Dölen: I believe, I mean, it's been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:19.360
long time since I was in medical school,&nbsp;
but last time I was, I looked into that. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.780 --> 00:24:30.360
believe it was still the case that FMR1 was&nbsp;
rare enough that it wasn't worth it to do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.360 --> 00:24:36.000
screening test to try and find it because&nbsp;
you have to balance doing a screening test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:41.160
against the possibility that the test itself&nbsp;
is going to cause problems with the pregnancy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:50.460
So there is a sort of a judgment call there and&nbsp;
unless you had a family history of Fragile X,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:59.100
I think that it's still the case that people&nbsp;
don't actually test for it beforehand. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.100 --> 00:25:07.440
not like Down syndrome also, where there's any&nbsp;
strong indication that — in Down syndrome there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:12.900
is a correlation between the age of the&nbsp;
mother and the likelihood of getting Down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.900 --> 00:25:18.900
syndrome. For schizophrenia, there seems&nbsp;
to be a correlation between the age of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:24.480
father and getting schizophrenia.
But for autism, as far as I know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:30.660
there is no age-dependent correlation with&nbsp;
your likelihood of getting it. And all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:37.380
the causes of autism are each individually&nbsp;
so rare that there's no screening test that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:43.620
is generally applicable that would be worth doing.
Tim Ferriss: Let's just revisit, as promised,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.620 --> 00:25:48.240
the reopening of the critical period.
Dr. Gül Dölen: The idea of critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.240 --> 00:25:56.880
periods first came in 1935 by Konrad Lorenz, who&nbsp;
was a ethologist, who was studying imprinting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:56.880 --> 00:26:03.540
behavior in snow geese. So this is the behavior&nbsp;
that shortly after hatching, the animals will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:09.600
form a long lasting attachment typically to&nbsp;
their mother. But if the mother isn't around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:16.440
and instead it's sort of a motorized airplane, or&nbsp;
the scientist in that case, Konrad Lorenz himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.440 --> 00:26:22.140
then they'll form that attachment to that other&nbsp;
moving object in their vicinity. And there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:27.180
all these funny pictures online of Konrad&nbsp;
Lorenz being followed around by little snow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:31.003
geese because they formed this attachment.
Tim Ferriss: Wait, what are snow geese?&nbsp;

00:26:31.003 --> 00:26:35.460
Dr. Gül Dölen: They're just a type of geese.
Tim Ferriss: Okay, there we go. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:40.080
just wanted to figure out. Sounds like a&nbsp;
little Scandinavian dwarf or something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:43.260
All right, got it. Just a type of geese.
Dr. Gül Dölen: Type of geese, bird. So they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.260 --> 00:26:49.560
followed around by these birds. They're really&nbsp;
cute pictures. Anyway on this imprinting behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:57.960
though, if you wait, so if you wait until 48&nbsp;
hours after hatching, then they don't form this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:06.300
behavior. So this attachment that is only possible&nbsp;
within the first 48 hours of hatching and Konrad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.300 --> 00:27:14.160
Lorenz called that period of time when the animals&nbsp;
are extremely sensitive to their environment and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:20.280
conformed these long-lasting memories, he called&nbsp;
that window of time the critical period. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:28.020
critical periods since then have been described&nbsp;
for so many different things. So probably the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:33.780
one that most people will be familiar with is&nbsp;
the critical period for language learning. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:39.060
so much easier to learn your first language&nbsp;
that you learn as a child than if you later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:43.080
on try and pick up another language. It's&nbsp;
harder. You always have an accent. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:50.640
easy to incorporate that into your first language.
And so that's a critical period for language. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:58.380
what my lab did is that we knew from the human&nbsp;
literature that there was probably a critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.380 --> 00:28:04.680
period for social learning because we know, for&nbsp;
example, that teenagers are much more susceptible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:11.880
to peer pressure than adults are. We know that&nbsp;
they prioritize learning from their environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:18.300
from their social interactions much more so than&nbsp;
adults do. And that whatever social environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.300 --> 00:28:24.000
you grew up in is sort of your native social&nbsp;
environment. So that, for example, when I go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:30.240
to Japan, I always feel like a bear in a China&nbsp;
closet because I feel like I'm always insulting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.240 --> 00:28:37.500
somebody without really realizing it. Because I&nbsp;
didn't grow up in that social environment. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:44.400
so I don't know that it's extremely rude to hand&nbsp;
somebody something with one hand instead of two.&nbsp;

00:28:45.360 --> 00:28:51.180
These are the kinds of rules that I didn't grow&nbsp;
up exposed to. So it's a foreign social language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.180 --> 00:28:56.220
to me. So we had this intuition that there was&nbsp;
a critical period for social reward learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.220 --> 00:29:02.280
and that's what my lab discovered. We discovered&nbsp;
that in mice, just like in humans, there is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:09.060
window of time where the animals are learning from&nbsp;
their social environment much more strongly than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:14.640
when they are as adults. And we figured out a&nbsp;
bunch of different mechanisms relating to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:23.040
critical periods. Typically, the long, long&nbsp;
arc trying to understand critical periods has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:30.960
really been focused around this idea that critical&nbsp;
periods are great, but the fact that they close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:30.960 --> 00:29:39.000
means that it's really, really hard to treat brain&nbsp;
disorders in adults because the brain is no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:44.880
flexible and able to learn from its environment&nbsp;
in the same way as it was as a child. And so if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.880 --> 00:29:49.860
can understand the mechanisms, maybe we can figure&nbsp;
out ways to reopen them for therapeutic benefit.&nbsp;

00:29:49.860 --> 00:29:56.700
That's been the goal of neuroscience for the&nbsp;
last hundred years or so, is to try and find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.700 --> 00:30:03.660
that. And three Nobel prizes have been given for&nbsp;
mechanisms trying to figure this out because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:09.420
understood that this would be so important. But&nbsp;
I've got to say, when I was a graduate student,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:10.620 --> 00:30:17.520
this idea of finding the master key for unlocking&nbsp;
critical periods, I never believed in it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.520 --> 00:30:24.660
in fact, my lab and another lab at Harvard&nbsp;
were kind of in an intellectual debate with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:30.720
one another over this idea of, "Can there&nbsp;
be a master key?" And I was always like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:36.480
"No. Obviously anything that could do that to&nbsp;
the brain would either cause massive amnesia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:40.800
would cause seizures, and possibly could cause&nbsp;
the whole brain to melt." And I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:45.060
"There's no master key. Stop it. Forget&nbsp;
about it, Harvard, you're just wrong." Okay?&nbsp;

00:30:45.060 --> 00:30:54.540
And so what we've come up with now is&nbsp;
we started with the idea that, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.540 --> 00:31:04.380
MDMA is different from other psychedelics in that&nbsp;
it has this very prominent pro-social component&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:10.680
to it. People take MDMA and they want to cuddle,&nbsp;
they want to be sociable. Some people talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.680 --> 00:31:16.500
this incredible feeling of empathy and being&nbsp;
able to see other people's pain. It's just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:22.320
very pro-social type of psychedelic. In fact, some&nbsp;
people even want to call it an empathogen rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:29.100
than a straight up psychedelic. And so we started&nbsp;
with that idea that, "Okay, we're trying to reopen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.100 --> 00:31:34.440
a social critical period. We know it follows a&nbsp;
lot of the same rules as other critical periods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:42.780
but maybe MDMA is the way to do it." And in fact,&nbsp;
we did find evidence that MDMA reopens the social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:49.740
critical period, and it does so by restoring&nbsp;
oxytocin metaplasticity in the nucleus succumbs.&nbsp;

00:31:49.740 --> 00:31:54.300
And we loved this idea that that was the story&nbsp;
and whatever. Psychedelics, other psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:54.300 --> 00:32:01.200
probably wouldn't do it. That was our original&nbsp;
idea. But then again, mother nature, tricky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:06.660
So when we did the control experiments to&nbsp;
look at what other psychedelics are doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.660 --> 00:32:13.320
we found that all of the psychedelics reopened&nbsp;
this critical period, even though nobody is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.320 --> 00:32:19.200
taking LSD and doing a 60-person cuddle puddle.&nbsp;
Not that I've seen anyway, right? But LSD is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.920 --> 00:32:28.380
prominently a pro-social type of psychedelic.&nbsp;
Neither is ibogaine, neither is ketamine. And yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:29.160 --> 00:32:35.820
all of them are reopening this critical&nbsp;
period for social reward learning. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:41.700
so what this suggested to me immediately&nbsp;
was is that the whole social part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:48.720
MDMA story is a little bit of a red herring.
Really what psychedelics are are this master&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:53.460
key for unlocking critical periods.
There's not just one critical period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:53.460 --> 00:33:00.180
there are lots of different critical periods.&nbsp;
But we are beginning to get a sense that many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:00.180 --> 00:33:08.280
the critical periods close using the same sort of&nbsp;
governing principle, the same kind of molecules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:08.280 --> 00:33:13.620
if not the exact same molecules, will or&nbsp;
have been implicated in the closure of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:20.460
say, the visual critical period or the touch&nbsp;
critical period or the motor critical period.&nbsp;

00:33:21.720 --> 00:33:28.260
What's different is that the social critical&nbsp;
period is different from those in that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:33.360
not a sensory motor. It's an emotional critical&nbsp;
period that we think is encoded in a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:33.360 --> 00:33:38.040
part of the brain, the nucleus accumbens,&nbsp;
which is part of the limbic system. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:38.040 --> 00:33:42.900
different type of brain region than the classical&nbsp;
critical periods which have all been defined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:50.100
in the cortex. So we don't know, still don't&nbsp;
know, whether or not the rules are the same.&nbsp;

00:33:50.100 --> 00:33:55.980
But what we did know, what we did&nbsp;
figure out is that when we did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:56.580 --> 00:34:03.720
RNA sequencing or genetic profiling,&nbsp;
transcriptional profiling, of what happens to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.720 --> 00:34:12.780
this brain region when we give psychedelics and we&nbsp;
reopen this social critical period, the molecules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:12.780 --> 00:34:20.280
that get up and down regulated in response to&nbsp;
psychedelics, but not cocaine, share a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:20.280 --> 00:34:27.420
overlap in terms of their molecular identity&nbsp;
with the molecules that have been implicated in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:34.380
the closure of other types of critical&nbsp;
periods like vision, somatic sensation, motor.&nbsp;

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:43.980
So beginning to see lots of circumstantial&nbsp;
evidence that the psychedelics are all doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:43.980 --> 00:34:48.240
the same thing, they're reopening this&nbsp;
critical period, and they're doing it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:48.240 --> 00:34:55.380
a way that suggests that the social critical&nbsp;
period, the social part of it is irrelevant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.380 --> 00:35:03.180
and that this is really tapping into molecular&nbsp;
mechanisms that are conserved across all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:03.180 --> 00:35:08.640
different types of critical periods.
If that's true, then it suggests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:08.640 --> 00:35:14.580
that these drugs might be useful for&nbsp;
reopening all kinds of critical periods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.580 --> 00:35:19.380
including the visual critical periods, including&nbsp;
the touch critical periods, including the motor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:26.640
critical periods. That's the next direction&nbsp;
that the lab is heading, is testing the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:32.400
that psychedelics are in fact this master key&nbsp;
for unlocking lots of different critical periods.&nbsp;

00:35:32.400 --> 00:35:39.120
Tim Ferriss: Question back to autism. And, man,&nbsp;
must you get a lot of questions about autism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:39.120 --> 00:35:47.040
so my apologies. But I want to use that&nbsp;
example because — this will underscore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.040 --> 00:35:54.660
my ignorance in so many of these topics. But if&nbsp;
we're looking at a narrow definition of autism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.660 --> 00:36:01.800
so we're not succumbing to the scope creep&nbsp;
in DSM or elsewhere where suddenly everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:01.800 --> 00:36:09.960
and its cousin is autism, but focusing more&nbsp;
on the Fragile X and that type of narrower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:09.960 --> 00:36:15.720
more specific definition, when I then think&nbsp;
of a critical window, what is the interplay of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.720 --> 00:36:22.200
the genetics and the critical window when it is&nbsp;
untreated, when you don't have an intervention?&nbsp;

00:36:22.200 --> 00:36:26.640
Does that mean that there is a critical window&nbsp;
but it's narrower, or for whatever reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:33.900
the child is less adaptive in that critical&nbsp;
window? So it's not genetic determinism. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.900 --> 00:36:40.440
not like if you have this particular phenotype&nbsp;
you're destined to get autism and that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:46.320
unwind the clock or rewind the clock in a sense&nbsp;
with potentially something like psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:52.200
to give them a second shot on goal. I know I'm&nbsp;
asking this in probably a very clumsy way because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:59.160
I don't understand exactly why it would work,&nbsp;
but why would reopening the window help someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:05.040
potentially with autism if they weren't able to&nbsp;
capitalize on a similar window earlier themselves?&nbsp;

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:14.280
Dr. Gül Dölen: Autism is a little bit complicated&nbsp;
because it's a multifactorial disease. Let me just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:20.460
give a different example to help clarify&nbsp;
and maybe make it clearer what we think is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.460 --> 00:37:25.800
the way that psychedelic could be used for&nbsp;
autism. And that's with vision. One of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:25.800 --> 00:37:35.460
early observations of critical periods is that if&nbsp;
a child is born with bilateral cataracts and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:42.540
you remove those cataracts and they can see, but&nbsp;
if you wait and you don't remove those cataracts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:49.320
until they're age six or seven, then even though&nbsp;
the physical impediment to vision is gone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.980 --> 00:37:56.640
the brain cannot adjust to the visual inputs&nbsp;
anymore, and so the child remains blind forever.&nbsp;

00:37:57.180 --> 00:38:05.220
That's because there is a critical period&nbsp;
for setting the strength of the synapses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:05.220 --> 00:38:11.340
for visual inputs. Once that critical&nbsp;
period is closed, even though the primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:11.340 --> 00:38:19.080
impediment or the primary injury has been&nbsp;
resolved, the brain can't — it missed its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:19.080 --> 00:38:24.240
opportunity to wire properly for the visual&nbsp;
environment and so they'll be blind forever.&nbsp;

00:38:24.240 --> 00:38:30.060
The idea there is, if you missed the window and&nbsp;
the child didn't have their cataracts removed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:38.520
until they were seven or eight years old and you&nbsp;
paired cataract removal surgery with psychedelics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.520 --> 00:38:45.000
then the idea would be that — if psychedelics&nbsp;
can reopen visual critical periods, then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:51.840
idea would be that you would be able to restore&nbsp;
vision because you would be pairing the primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.840 --> 00:38:57.360
intervention, which is the cataract removal&nbsp;
surgery, with the reopening of the critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:57.360 --> 00:39:02.280
period to give the opportunity to learn from&nbsp;
the visual environment in the natural way.&nbsp;

00:39:03.660 --> 00:39:10.080
With autism, it's the same idea. We're saying,&nbsp;
"Okay, there's a genetic impairment that causes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:16.980
autism which we think is related to an imbalance&nbsp;
at this one specific receptor, which we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:24.900
correct by targeting that receptor. But if we just&nbsp;
do that alone after the social critical period has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:24.900 --> 00:39:32.220
closed, then they still remain socially blind,&nbsp;
if you will, because we missed that window." So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:32.220 --> 00:39:39.720
the idea is if we could pair that mGluR therapy&nbsp;
with a psychedelic, then we could restore the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:46.800
imbalance and also restore the ability to learn&nbsp;
from the social environment so that they can,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.800 --> 00:39:52.740
under those corrected conditions, have a shot at&nbsp;
learning the ways of social interaction the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:52.740 --> 00:39:55.980
they would've during natural development.
Tim Ferriss: Got it. Thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:55.980 --> 00:40:02.340
you. That's very helpful.
I want to just perhaps mention that — and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:02.340 --> 00:40:06.420
is actually from The Microdose, which is a great&nbsp;
newsletter. You were featured in The Microdose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:06.420 --> 00:40:16.320
from UC Berkeley not too long ago. Some people&nbsp;
listening may wonder why we shouldn't always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:16.320 --> 00:40:21.540
leave our critical windows open. Wouldn't that be&nbsp;
great? We could learn everything and anything. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:26.820
just want to read something here and then we&nbsp;
can move on, although we could expand on it.&nbsp;

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:29.340
When you talk to people who are on&nbsp;
psychedelics, it's like herding kittens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:29.340 --> 00:40:33.290
They're paying attention to everything. "Look&nbsp;
at this leaf," "My shoe won't fit," et cetera.&nbsp;

00:40:33.290 --> 00:40:37.440
You're in full explorer mode, which is wonderful,&nbsp;
but it's not very efficient. Habits get a bad rap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:40.620
but you couldn't get out the door if you didn't&nbsp;
have them. Living in a world where you're trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:40.620 --> 00:40:44.160
to learn and pay attention to everything&nbsp;
is emotionally and energetically costly.&nbsp;

00:40:46.200 --> 00:40:48.900
We could certainly expand on&nbsp;
this, but needless to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:49.620 --> 00:40:57.660
there is a lot to be said for a stable version of&nbsp;
reality with certain scripts that run reliably. Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:57.660 --> 00:41:05.040
there anything you would like to add to that?
Dr. Gül Dölen: I would just expand on that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.040 --> 00:41:11.160
little bit by pointing out that the reason&nbsp;
that we have critical periods at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:16.620
rather than being genetically encoded with&nbsp;
everything that we need to survive in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.620 --> 00:41:24.360
is because the world is big and varied.&nbsp;
If you genetically encoded everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:24.360 --> 00:41:32.520
you probably would be very limited in how you&nbsp;
could navigate that world. My parents are Turkish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.520 --> 00:41:38.220
If I was genetically encoded with Turkish,&nbsp;
the fact that I grew up in the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:43.980
it would make my life very difficult because I&nbsp;
wouldn't speak English, I would speak Turkish.&nbsp;

00:41:43.980 --> 00:41:52.740
But because I have a language critical period,&nbsp;
what's genetically encoded is the ability to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:52.740 --> 00:41:58.980
a language, not the language itself. That ability&nbsp;
to learn from our environment is why we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:58.980 --> 00:42:10.500
them. But as everybody knows, being a teenager&nbsp;
sucks. It's hard. It's painful. It's really very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:10.500 --> 00:42:18.540
emotionally draining to constantly be checking&nbsp;
yourself, trying to figure out how you fit in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:19.200 --> 00:42:26.100
Sometimes, you want to just get on with it, and&nbsp;
if the environment that you're in is stable, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:26.820 --> 00:42:32.700
you don't need to necessarily be adapting&nbsp;
or adjusting your model of the world every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:32.700 --> 00:42:36.720
single move that you make. That's why&nbsp;
we think that critical periods close.&nbsp;

00:42:36.720 --> 00:42:41.880
But then another question is, well, why does&nbsp;
the brain have a sort of readymade mechanism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:41.880 --> 00:42:48.240
that psychedelics can tap into for reopening&nbsp;
critical periods? Why can we reopen them so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:48.240 --> 00:42:54.840
easily if the brain wasn't already built to&nbsp;
do this? This is really me speculating, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.840 --> 00:43:02.520
suspect it's because when we have these habits,&nbsp;
they're adaptive so long as the environment is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:02.520 --> 00:43:08.400
stable. But then there's a radical shift in the&nbsp;
environment, then you can imagine that it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:13.380
be adaptive to be able to reopen critical&nbsp;
periods and relearn from your environment.&nbsp;

00:43:13.380 --> 00:43:19.200
Going back to the language example, as long as&nbsp;
everybody around you speaks English, it's probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:19.200 --> 00:43:26.880
not all that adaptive to be learning Spanish.&nbsp;
But if you move to Spain or you move to Central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:26.880 --> 00:43:34.020
America, then suddenly nobody's speaking English,&nbsp;
and you want to be able to learn the language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:34.020 --> 00:43:40.800
that everybody's speaking around you. So we have a&nbsp;
little bit of other evidence that this deprivation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:47.520
technique of reopening critical periods, which has&nbsp;
been described for things like the visual system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:47.520 --> 00:43:53.460
the sensory system, a little bit in the&nbsp;
motor system, there's evidence for this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:53.460 --> 00:43:58.260
that this deprivation induced can also&nbsp;
reopen our social critical period.&nbsp;

00:43:58.980 --> 00:44:06.180
Here's another rule that applies across critical&nbsp;
periods, is that deprivation is a technique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:11.580
This might be the explanation, for example,&nbsp;
for why it's easier to learn Spanish if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:11.580 --> 00:44:17.220
go to Guatemala than if you go to Costa Rica,&nbsp;
because in Costa Rica everybody speaks Spanish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:17.220 --> 00:44:21.840
but everybody also speaks English. So it's&nbsp;
very hard. You're not deprived of English,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:21.840 --> 00:44:26.940
you're just exposed to Spanish. Whereas in&nbsp;
Guatemala, almost nobody speaks English and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:26.940 --> 00:44:31.980
so you're deprived and you get the language.
That's one idea that we have. We haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:39.000
proven it. But the other part of that&nbsp;
is that it's interesting to note that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:39.720 --> 00:44:48.600
historically, religious practices across many,&nbsp;
many different cultures have used deprivation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:55.380
techniques to get to that mystical state&nbsp;
that people talk about. Zen Buddhist call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:55.380 --> 00:45:01.740
it beginner's mind. If you were looking for a&nbsp;
neurobiological description of beginner's mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:01.740 --> 00:45:09.660
reopening critical periods would be it.
So what we think, possibly, is that — remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:09.660 --> 00:45:16.920
how I said that I was as an undergrad, I was&nbsp;
really interested in getting around to using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:16.920 --> 00:45:22.920
neuroscience to answer the really big questions&nbsp;
that philosophers seem to have exclusive domain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:22.920 --> 00:45:30.840
for? Well, this might be it, right? It might be&nbsp;
that if what it feels like to be in that altered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.840 --> 00:45:39.180
state of consciousness or that mystical experience&nbsp;
that we think of with psychedelics is just what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:39.180 --> 00:45:46.920
it feels like to reopen critical periods.
Then this explanation, this critical period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:46.920 --> 00:45:52.740
explanation, gives us a mechanistic handle&nbsp;
to start answering those big questions like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:52.740 --> 00:45:58.200
well, what is consciousness? Well, what is&nbsp;
that mystical experience? What does it mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:58.200 --> 00:46:02.880
to feel you're an altered state?
Tim Ferriss: In the course of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:03.540 --> 00:46:09.960
my reading, and feel free to correct anything&nbsp;
that I misstate here, but part of what makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:11.400 --> 00:46:15.660
catalyzing pro-social behavior&nbsp;
in octopuses is so interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:16.680 --> 00:46:25.800
with the use of, say, MDMA is that the nervous&nbsp;
system of an octopus and the anatomical structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:25.800 --> 00:46:31.440
sorry, as compared to humans are so different.&nbsp;
So in humans we might attribute some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:31.440 --> 00:46:37.200
outcomes that we see or therapeutic effects to,&nbsp;
I believe the nucleus accumbens. But then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:37.200 --> 00:46:41.940
look at an octopus, no accumbens, no amygdala,&nbsp;
no default mode network, no prefrontal cortex.&nbsp;

00:46:41.940 --> 00:46:45.660
Dr. Gül Dölen: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: What have you gleaned from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.660 --> 00:46:53.760
octopus work as it relates to critical periods&nbsp;
and or interesting characteristics of MDMA?&nbsp;

00:46:53.760 --> 00:46:59.820
Dr. Gül Dölen: Tim, I'm really glad that you asked&nbsp;
that question because I think that possibly a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:59.820 --> 00:47:05.760
of people in your audience know a little something&nbsp;
about psychedelics and they've seen the studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:05.760 --> 00:47:12.660
where people have been given psychedelics and they&nbsp;
take an fMRI image and they point to this brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:12.660 --> 00:47:19.380
region or that brain region and say, "Aha, this is&nbsp;
where psychedelics are working." And they get this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:19.380 --> 00:47:25.560
sort of warm and fuzzy feeling like, "I understand&nbsp;
now what's happening." And as a neuroscientist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:25.560 --> 00:47:31.500
I am susceptible to that interpretation as&nbsp;
well. I don't do it with fMRI. I do it with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:32.160 --> 00:47:36.780
circuit mapping and viral mediated gene&nbsp;
transfer and look at it at a much higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:36.780 --> 00:47:42.780
resolution than fMRI imaging allows, so my lab,&nbsp;
we do whole-cell patch clamp electrophysiology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:42.780 --> 00:47:49.800
so we can do single neuron resolution of these&nbsp;
kinds of anatomical maps of what neurons are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:49.800 --> 00:47:53.280
getting turned on by psychedelics.
And from those types of analyses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:53.280 --> 00:48:02.040
we get this satisfying feeling like, "Aha, MDMA&nbsp;
is causing the nucleus accumbens neurons to light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:02.040 --> 00:48:09.240
up. It's causing the oxytocin neurons and the&nbsp;
hypothalamus to fire and release oxytocin. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:09.240 --> 00:48:16.020
got it. We understand how these work." And I&nbsp;
think the really, really compelling thing about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:16.020 --> 00:48:24.060
the octopus study and why so many people who&nbsp;
are not lay people, just regular scientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:24.060 --> 00:48:31.620
we're a little bit shaken by this octopus paper,&nbsp;
is because it challenges that very comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:31.620 --> 00:48:37.740
place that we've all been sitting with circuit&nbsp;
mapping that we have an answer. And what it says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:37.740 --> 00:48:46.200
instead is that, yeah, okay, those anatomical&nbsp;
results matter and they're telling us something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:46.200 --> 00:48:53.760
but they're not telling us the general rules&nbsp;
of how you build complex behavioral functions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:53.760 --> 00:48:59.700
from synapses, circuits, brain regions.
And instead what they're telling us is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:59.700 --> 00:49:08.820
something like the historical accident or the&nbsp;
evolutionary byproduct of one animal's history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:08.820 --> 00:49:13.980
of how they came up with this solution. But we&nbsp;
haven't really learned anything about what are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:13.980 --> 00:49:21.480
the generalizable principles? What are the motifs?&nbsp;
What are the rules governing those circuit level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:28.800
rearrangements that enable complex behavior?
Tim Ferriss: What are some other ideas in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:28.800 --> 00:49:35.940
mind that need challenging or that could be&nbsp;
challenged, just writ large in psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:36.600 --> 00:49:40.440
or psychedelic-related science?
Dr. Gül Dölen: I think the two big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:40.440 --> 00:49:47.700
ones that I have, let me start with — since we're&nbsp;
already beating up on the people who do human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:47.700 --> 00:49:54.420
research a little bit, let me just challenge the&nbsp;
idea that we can only understand what psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:54.420 --> 00:50:00.900
are doing in humans. That I think for the&nbsp;
last decade and the early part of this one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:00.900 --> 00:50:06.720
there has been this idea that really the cool&nbsp;
and interesting part of psychedelics, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:06.720 --> 00:50:14.760
only understand by studying it in humans, because&nbsp;
humans are the only ones who do the behaviorally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:14.760 --> 00:50:21.180
complex enough things that you would want to know&nbsp;
about what psychedelics are doing. And I consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:21.180 --> 00:50:28.560
Alexander Shulgin to be an elder in this field for&nbsp;
me. I met him when he came to visit MIT. I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:28.560 --> 00:50:36.480
this goofy picture of myself as this young little&nbsp;
neuroscientist, all enamored of Alexander Shulgin.&nbsp;

00:50:36.480 --> 00:50:41.040
And I think he was very much a proponent of this&nbsp;
view that he needed to take the psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:41.040 --> 00:50:47.700
himself because really, the interesting part of&nbsp;
what psychedelics are doing, you can only know on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:47.700 --> 00:50:55.080
a personal level. And I want to push back against&nbsp;
that idea because I think that as we start to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:55.080 --> 00:51:01.980
a better handle of what they're actually doing in&nbsp;
mice, we may be able to come up with things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:01.980 --> 00:51:08.400
generalize. Because after all, humans didn't just&nbsp;
emerge out of nowhere. We weren't plopped here on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:08.400 --> 00:51:16.260
earth by aliens. We evolved just all other&nbsp;
animals on Earth. And so we were subject to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:16.260 --> 00:51:24.960
a lot of the same rules and we came up with some&nbsp;
mostly overlapping solutions and some unique ones.&nbsp;

00:51:24.960 --> 00:51:30.600
And so I think we will eventually get to a point&nbsp;
where we can study the really cool and interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:30.600 --> 00:51:37.920
parts of what psychedelics are doing in animals as&nbsp;
well as in humans. And I think that our critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:37.920 --> 00:51:42.780
period idea is one step in that direction.
I think that many people in your audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:42.780 --> 00:51:47.340
may have also heard of the idea that&nbsp;
psychedelics are inducing plasticity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:47.340 --> 00:51:55.020
And I'm very careful not to use that word if I&nbsp;
can avoid it, because plasticity is a word that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:55.020 --> 00:52:00.480
is thrown around a lot and it means a lot of&nbsp;
different things to a lot of different people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:00.480 --> 00:52:10.260
So for a synaptic neurophysiologist, it has a very&nbsp;
specific meaning. It's about the something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:10.260 --> 00:52:18.720
you can record from doing whole-cell recordings&nbsp;
or patch-clamp recordings or even unit recordings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:18.720 --> 00:52:25.620
in the brain. But it's an electrophysiological&nbsp;
property that is defined between two synapses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:26.220 --> 00:52:32.460
Whereas the clinicians basically use plasticity&nbsp;
to mean anything that changes over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:38.880
which is so general, it sort of loses meaning.
And so I try not to use that word as much as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:38.880 --> 00:52:45.600
possible, and especially in the context of&nbsp;
psychedelics, because some people have tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:45.600 --> 00:52:53.280
to make the argument that what psychedelics&nbsp;
are so-called psychoplastogens or drugs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:53.280 --> 00:53:01.560
induce plasticity. And this is an inaccurate and&nbsp;
confusing terminology that I would really hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:01.560 --> 00:53:08.520
we could abandon because there are drugs that&nbsp;
cause plasticity in the way that word implies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:09.180 --> 00:53:17.640
And they're all drugs of abuse. So heroin,&nbsp;
nicotine, cocaine, amphetamine, these are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:17.640 --> 00:53:24.720
psychoactive drugs that cause robust bidirectional&nbsp;
plasticity. They cause the neurons to sprout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:24.720 --> 00:53:30.540
they cause the neurons to shrink, they cause&nbsp;
robust changes. And it's that very plastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:30.540 --> 00:53:39.780
or hyperplastic property that we think underlies&nbsp;
their addictive potential because that exaggerated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:39.780 --> 00:53:47.460
sprouting or that exaggerated plasticity,&nbsp;
we think is what enables drugs of abuse to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:48.960 --> 00:53:58.560
override the breaks on learning and memory&nbsp;
that are normally part of naturally rewarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:58.560 --> 00:54:05.520
stimuli that allow memories to be&nbsp;
encoded in a way that are driven by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:06.060 --> 00:54:08.640
limitations on association.
So let me give you an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:08.640 --> 00:54:15.660
because I'm fumbling a little. So the example I&nbsp;
like to give is that a natural reward, like food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:15.660 --> 00:54:24.060
for example. We like food. And if I like a, let's&nbsp;
say, a turkey sandwich, I might remember the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:24.060 --> 00:54:29.520
memory that I formed to a turkey sandwich that&nbsp;
I just ate and loved would be something like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:29.520 --> 00:54:36.420
"Oh, I love that turkey sandwich. Next time I'm&nbsp;
hungry and I'm in this neighborhood, then I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:36.420 --> 00:54:41.280
going to go to that store and buy that sandwich&nbsp;
again. I loved it." But for drugs of abuse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:41.280 --> 00:54:51.000
we override those associative constraints on&nbsp;
when we want the thing. And a drug of abuse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:51.000 --> 00:54:56.400
cocaine or nicotine, it doesn't matter if you're&nbsp;
hungry or you're in the right neighborhood or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:57.120 --> 00:55:04.320
it's not out of your way. You just like it always.
And that very ability to override that constraint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:04.320 --> 00:55:12.180
is what we think the hyperplasticity is encoding&nbsp;
and enabling and making those drugs so much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:12.180 --> 00:55:18.420
addictive. And so I wouldn't want people&nbsp;
to confuse that property, that hyperplastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:18.420 --> 00:55:24.600
property with what we think is actually happening&nbsp;
with psychedelics, which is that psychedelics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:24.600 --> 00:55:29.100
we believe, and there's starting to be&nbsp;
other evidence to support this view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:29.940 --> 00:55:36.300
are enabling what's called metaplasticity.&nbsp;
Which I know that it's confusing terminology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:36.300 --> 00:55:42.600
hyperplasticity versus metaplasticity. But&nbsp;
metaplasticity is an idea that's been around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:42.600 --> 00:55:53.700
for over 20 years now that says that the ability&nbsp;
to induce plasticity also can change over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:53.700 --> 00:56:01.080
typically. So when you're young, your ability&nbsp;
to induce plasticity is greater than when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:01.080 --> 00:56:08.520
older. The ability to induce plasticity declines&nbsp;
and there are molecular things that control that.&nbsp;

00:56:08.520 --> 00:56:11.640
Tim Ferriss: You were mentioning that&nbsp;
plasticity can mean a lot of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:11.640 --> 00:56:16.020
things to different people. So in this&nbsp;
case, the ability to induce plasticity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:16.020 --> 00:56:22.140
what does plasticity mean to you in this use?
Dr. Gül Dölen: So the metaplastic definition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:22.140 --> 00:56:29.760
I mean it's a very specific definition, plasticity&nbsp;
of plasticity, and in that case it was defined by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:30.660 --> 00:56:39.600
Mark Bear and Cliff Abraham, and it was a synaptic&nbsp;
physiology. So it's synaptic plasticity. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:39.600 --> 00:56:47.280
change in response of stimulating one synapse and&nbsp;
the magnitude of the response changing when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:47.280 --> 00:56:48.883
recording the post synapse.
Tim Ferriss: Got it.&nbsp;

00:56:48.883 --> 00:56:56.700
Dr. Gül Dölen: So very narrowly defined again.&nbsp;
Metaplasticity originally was used to define&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:56.700 --> 00:57:03.060
what happens in the reopening or the closure&nbsp;
of the critical period for ocular dominance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:03.060 --> 00:57:12.360
plasticity. And it was defined primarily in terms&nbsp;
of the change in the subunit composition of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:12.360 --> 00:57:18.960
NMDA receptor, which is one of the receptors&nbsp;
that's been so heavily implicated in learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:18.960 --> 00:57:25.380
in memory and specifically also learning in&nbsp;
memory with regards to visual processing.&nbsp;

00:57:25.380 --> 00:57:32.100
Tim Ferriss: Does that mean that ketamine&nbsp;
would have any particular applications to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:33.480 --> 00:57:40.560
studies involving visual processing? I'm just&nbsp;
thinking given the — seems like there's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:40.560 --> 00:57:47.580
decent effect at the NMDA receptor level with&nbsp;
ketamine, when you look at chronic pain and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:47.580 --> 00:57:51.203
other things. Is that the case or is not so much?
Dr. Gül Dölen: I mean, yes, almost certainly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:51.203 --> 00:57:53.640
Almost certainly the NMDA receptors, there are&nbsp;
a variety of things that have been attributed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:53.640 --> 00:57:57.300
to the NMDA receptor. For a long time it&nbsp;
was my favorite receptor, and here's why.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:58.740 --> 00:58:01.487
I know it's super nerdy to&nbsp;
have a favorite receptor, but —&nbsp;

00:58:01.487 --> 00:58:06.000
Tim Ferriss: No. I'm into it. I'm into it.
Dr. Gül Dölen: So NMDA receptors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:07.020 --> 00:58:13.560
fast neurotransmitter receptors, but they're&nbsp;
different from AMPA receptors, which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:21.420
the other major subtype of glutamate receptor&nbsp;
because they have something that basically the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:21.420 --> 00:58:29.460
way the receptor is designed is that it's blocked&nbsp;
usually by magnesium. And only when the neuron is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:30.240 --> 00:58:36.540
active or the membrane potential changes and&nbsp;
the magnesium no longer wants to sit in there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:36.540 --> 00:58:43.740
that magnesium comes out. So because of that&nbsp;
property, the NMDA receptor is thought to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:43.740 --> 00:58:50.520
a natural coincidence detector. So it can detect&nbsp;
when two neurons are firing at the same time. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:50.520 --> 00:58:57.300
that has gotten neuroscientists super excited&nbsp;
about the idea that this is exactly the type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:57.300 --> 00:59:06.240
coincidence detection that would be necessary at&nbsp;
a cellular level in order to encode memories. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:06.240 --> 00:59:12.120
this idea has been around for a while now. It's&nbsp;
why people are excited about the NMDA receptor.&nbsp;

00:59:12.120 --> 00:59:22.200
Incidentally, it's also thought to be why ketamine&nbsp;
and PCP interfere with memory. So they do cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:22.200 --> 00:59:27.120
impairments in memory because they mess with a&nbsp;
receptor that we think is super important for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:27.120 --> 00:59:34.620
encoding memories. Now, in terms of this critical&nbsp;
period idea, NMDA receptors, they come in, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:34.620 --> 00:59:41.580
made up of different subunits, and the composition&nbsp;
of those subunits changes over development. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:41.580 --> 00:59:50.220
very early in development, the NMDA receptors have&nbsp;
the subunit, the B subunit is more predominant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:50.220 --> 00:59:57.960
And the way I remember that is that that's the&nbsp;
NR2B subunit is for baby, and then the A subunit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:57.960 --> 01:00:04.440
is for adults. So as the brain matures, then the&nbsp;
subunit switches out and it goes from being 2B&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:04.440 --> 01:00:11.400
heavy to being 2A heavy. And what that does to&nbsp;
the receptor is it makes it more or less likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:12.120 --> 01:00:18.480
to be able to induce that synaptic plasticity&nbsp;
when the glutamate binds to that receptor.&nbsp;

01:00:18.480 --> 01:00:24.900
And that is the original first classical&nbsp;
understanding of what a metaplastic change&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:24.900 --> 01:00:30.360
would look like. It would be the change in the&nbsp;
subunit composition of the NMDA receptor that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:30.360 --> 01:00:35.880
enabled the baby receptors to induce plasticity&nbsp;
more readily than the adult receptors do.&nbsp;

01:00:35.880 --> 01:00:44.280
Tim Ferriss: Got it. And just to try to recap&nbsp;
my understanding, which will hopefully help&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:44.280 --> 01:00:53.700
the audience track as well. Jumping back to&nbsp;
the autism example and the Fragile X or FMR1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:54.420 --> 01:01:04.140
the joint therapy would look like using the&nbsp;
compounds such as, possibly a psychedelic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:04.140 --> 01:01:14.700
to increase the metaplasticity followed by, or&nbsp;
maybe simultaneous, with the administration of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:14.700 --> 01:01:21.480
drug that was so disappointing in human trials&nbsp;
because it was administered to adults who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:21.480 --> 01:01:27.291
already switched to a different dominant subtype.&nbsp;
I'm getting close, I'm not quite there, but —&nbsp;

01:01:27.291 --> 01:01:32.760
Dr. Gül Dölen: You're very close and you're&nbsp;
actually picking up on something that is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:32.760 --> 01:01:39.480
hesitation I have about using psychedelics in&nbsp;
autism. So there's another, a third type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:40.080 --> 01:01:46.920
glutamate receptor that I love. It's called the&nbsp;
metabotropic glutamate receptor, the mGluR and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:46.920 --> 01:01:55.380
the metabotropic glutamate receptor, we think is&nbsp;
the one whose signaling is so altered in Fragile&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:55.380 --> 01:02:03.360
X. And the metabotropic glutamate receptors are&nbsp;
also implicated in — as their brain matures, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:03.360 --> 01:02:10.440
expression goes down. And so we think that that&nbsp;
coincides with the closure of the critical period.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:10.440 --> 01:02:20.280
And what we think is that in Fragile X, that mGluR&nbsp;
signaling is exaggerated. It's not being curtailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:20.280 --> 01:02:27.120
in the right way. And so we have some evidence to&nbsp;
suggest that autism might be caused by a failure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:27.120 --> 01:02:33.900
to properly close the critical periods. So even&nbsp;
though theoretically I like the idea of combining&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:34.620 --> 01:02:42.360
negative allosteric modulator of the mGluR with&nbsp;
a psychedelic that can reopen, say the social&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:42.360 --> 01:02:46.920
critical periods through metaplasticity&nbsp;
in this case of a different receptor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:46.920 --> 01:02:51.000
I think in this case it would be the oxytocin&nbsp;
receptor that would be the relevant one.&nbsp;

01:02:51.660 --> 01:02:58.440
I am a little bit hesitant to jump into human&nbsp;
trials for that without first testing the safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:58.440 --> 01:03:05.280
of this in mouse models because again, there's&nbsp;
this tension between, there's some idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:05.280 --> 01:03:11.580
the pathogenesis of the disease is related to a&nbsp;
failure to close the critical periods properly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:11.580 --> 01:03:16.500
So I'm not sure that reopening them later is&nbsp;
going to be necessarily the right way to go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:16.500 --> 01:03:23.280
So it's still a hypothesis that we're testing.
Tim Ferriss: How do allergies fit into this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:24.240 --> 01:03:30.780
I've become very, very interested&nbsp;
in allergies and all sorts of —&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:30.780 --> 01:03:36.960
not to paint with too broad a brush, but sort of&nbsp;
autoimmune questions specifically as related to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:37.560 --> 01:03:43.680
psychedelic compounds. How do allergies fit in?
Dr. Gül Dölen: Well, first I should premise this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:43.680 --> 01:03:51.780
by telling you that my mom is an immunologist&nbsp;
and she hates this idea. So she and I we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:51.780 --> 01:03:58.140
spar over this and we have a very different view&nbsp;
of the relationship between the immune system&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:58.140 --> 01:04:04.380
and the nervous system. She basically lives&nbsp;
in an immune centric world where the nervous&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:04.380 --> 01:04:10.680
system is just like an downstream effector of the&nbsp;
immune system, which is the primary controller of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:10.680 --> 01:04:18.180
everything. And obviously I think it's the other&nbsp;
way around. So the idea with allergy is this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:20.160 --> 01:04:28.260
there is some epidemiological evidence to suggest&nbsp;
that allergy has a higher prevalence in countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:28.260 --> 01:04:35.220
where the food and the water is largely parasite&nbsp;
free. And so that part of the immune system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:35.220 --> 01:04:45.420
which is ordinarily used to fight off parasitic&nbsp;
infections, is left jobless. So the idea is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:45.420 --> 01:04:52.140
in that state of joblessness, it sort of goes&nbsp;
out looking for another threat to fight against,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:52.140 --> 01:05:00.060
to protect the body against. And in this&nbsp;
formulation, I am thinking how that threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:00.060 --> 01:05:04.920
gets assigned is really through the brain.
The brain is saying, "Okay, well that dog&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:04.920 --> 01:05:11.700
is barking at you and your immune system is&nbsp;
left jobless, that's threatening. Go fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:11.700 --> 01:05:17.640
against the dog proteins." And if that's true,&nbsp;
at least in a subset of patients with allergy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:17.640 --> 01:05:27.180
then you can imagine that reopening that&nbsp;
learned sort of threat, that critical period&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:27.180 --> 01:05:33.960
for the learned threat of that's mis-assigned,&nbsp;
would enable people to unlearn the association&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:33.960 --> 01:05:42.060
between dog and allergy. Now that's a very&nbsp;
different way, for example, of looking at it than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:43.140 --> 01:05:49.740
the way that some people focus on psychedelics to&nbsp;
think of it as they have these anti-inflammatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:49.740 --> 01:05:57.240
properties, which I think are true independent&nbsp;
of this critical period explanation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:58.140 --> 01:06:07.800
that property might be beneficial in allergy,&nbsp;
independent of this learned association&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:08.700 --> 01:06:14.100
framework that I'm proposing around the&nbsp;
critical period. So we just don't know yet.&nbsp;

01:06:14.100 --> 01:06:21.360
I like the idea though, because there are&nbsp;
a few anecdotal stories of, I think Andrew&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:21.360 --> 01:06:25.500
Weil has a story where he tears himself up.
Tim Ferriss: I was going to bring him up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:26.040 --> 01:06:28.920
Cats. Yeah.
Dr. Gül Dölen: And I mean, I love the idea because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:28.920 --> 01:06:35.520
I actually kind of wanted to be a veterinarian,&nbsp;
but I'm allergic to horses, dogs, and cats. So I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:35.520 --> 01:06:40.560
would love to take psychedelics and cure myself&nbsp;
of those allergies. And while I love this idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:40.560 --> 01:06:49.380
I don't believe it enough yet to take a bunch of&nbsp;
psychedelics and go snuggle up to a horse because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:49.380 --> 01:06:54.900
I think we could be looking at anaphylactic shock&nbsp;
in that situation. So I don't believe it yet, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:54.900 --> 01:07:01.080
I like the idea as something that we could test.
Tim Ferriss: Okay, I'm glad you brought up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:01.080 --> 01:07:08.580
Andy and not the sole example. I mean I have an&nbsp;
understanding the plural of anecdote is not data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:08.580 --> 01:07:15.360
but nonetheless sometimes helpful for generating&nbsp;
hypotheses. This is something that's been in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:16.740 --> 01:07:21.900
conversation with numerous anecdotes for a&nbsp;
while. And I guess what I want to ask next&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:21.900 --> 01:07:28.200
is whether it's autism, stroke, or allergies?&nbsp;
And I do want to come back to stroke. When we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:28.200 --> 01:07:35.760
are talking about reopening critical windows,&nbsp;
and in the introduction in your bio that I read,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:35.760 --> 01:07:42.840
we had MDMA, LSD, psilocybin, ketamine, and&nbsp;
ibogaine, which act on quite a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:42.840 --> 01:07:48.180
different receptors. LSD seems particularly&nbsp;
promiscuous, maybe ibogaine as well. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:50.700 --> 01:07:58.440
broadly speaking, are we talking about increasing&nbsp;
post-synaptic sensitivity? In which case they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:58.440 --> 01:08:07.080
could be hitting different receptors, but as long&nbsp;
as it's eliciting that type of sensitivity that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:07.080 --> 01:08:13.620
associated with earlier critical window, it fits&nbsp;
the description of reopening the critical window.&nbsp;

01:08:13.620 --> 01:08:19.860
Dr. Gül Dölen: So let me back up for a second and&nbsp;
just explain that I think there's a debate going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:19.860 --> 01:08:25.920
on in the field right now because if you look&nbsp;
back in the textbooks written in the '70s and '80s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:25.920 --> 01:08:32.460
about psychedelics, textbooks written by people&nbsp;
like Solomon Snyder, who started my department&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:32.460 --> 01:08:37.800
at Johns Hopkins and discovered almost every&nbsp;
neurotransmitter receptor you've ever heard of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:37.800 --> 01:08:45.900
including, by the way, the serotonin 2A receptor.&nbsp;
Their definition of what a psychedelic is was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:45.900 --> 01:08:52.740
very broad. So they basically said it's anything&nbsp;
that causes this altered state of consciousness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:53.640 --> 01:09:02.400
altered perception of time, space, body, self,&nbsp;
these were all sort of things that psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:02.400 --> 01:09:10.800
do. And they included psychedelics ranging from&nbsp;
ketamine, PCP, all of what people are now trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:10.800 --> 01:09:18.060
to call the classical serotonin psychedelics&nbsp;
like LSD, mescaline, psilocybin, DMT, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:18.060 --> 01:09:26.340
also the weird ones like MDMA and ibogaine.
And both Snyder and Alexander Shulgin consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:26.340 --> 01:09:34.080
all of those drugs to be psychedelics. More&nbsp;
recently, there has been this push mostly led by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:34.080 --> 01:09:40.560
the chemists to say, "Oh, no, no, no. If we look&nbsp;
at the receptor binding properties of these drugs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:40.560 --> 01:09:46.380
the classical psychedelics, the hallucinogenic&nbsp;
psychedelics, they're all serotonin 2A receptor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:46.380 --> 01:09:50.400
binding. And even though they're promiscuous, even&nbsp;
though they're binding to a bunch of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:50.400 --> 01:09:59.280
receptors, the psychedelic effects seem to be&nbsp;
mediated by this receptor using pharmacology&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:59.280 --> 01:10:07.440
studies in humans." And they are really pushing&nbsp;
hard to exclude MDMA and ibogaine and ketamine&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:07.440 --> 01:10:13.020
from the definition of classical psychedelics.&nbsp;
But I personally feel that this is putting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:13.020 --> 01:10:20.400
cart before the horse because we don't know&nbsp;
that that is necessarily a good boundary for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:20.940 --> 01:10:25.740
defining psychedelics. First of all, because&nbsp;
we know that most of the psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:25.740 --> 01:10:31.020
are very promiscuous in their binding.
Second of all, we don't really understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:31.020 --> 01:10:40.080
whether or not the 2A receptor is required for the&nbsp;
therapeutic properties. So there is some evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:40.080 --> 01:10:46.680
that there are anti-depressant properties that&nbsp;
may or may not be blocked by serotonin receptor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:46.680 --> 01:10:53.580
antagonists. There's two papers that have exactly&nbsp;
opposite results about this right now. I am not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:53.580 --> 01:10:58.860
entirely convinced that those antidepressant&nbsp;
properties that they're measuring have anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:58.860 --> 01:11:05.640
to do with the durable context dependent long-term&nbsp;
therapeutic effects of psychedelics that we see in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:05.640 --> 01:11:13.020
humans. And so I think it's up for debate whether&nbsp;
or not the serotonin 2A receptor is required. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:13.020 --> 01:11:23.700
what we do know is that psychedelics that have&nbsp;
primary binding at NMDA receptors, serotonin&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:23.700 --> 01:11:30.300
transporters, serotonin 2A receptors, kappa-opioid&nbsp;
receptors, ones that span the diversity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:30.300 --> 01:11:37.140
receptors all show some therapeutic effect.
And so our lab has really been focused in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:37.140 --> 01:11:41.340
trying to understand, "Well, what's&nbsp;
the commonality across all of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:41.340 --> 01:11:45.960
receptors?" And so to, in a long-winded&nbsp;
way, get back to your original question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:47.340 --> 01:11:52.860
what we wanted to know is what's downstream of&nbsp;
those receptors that's common to all of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:52.860 --> 01:11:57.540
psychedelics. And we don't have the complete&nbsp;
answer yet, but the answer that we're coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:57.540 --> 01:12:03.480
up upon is that it's not the receptor. It's&nbsp;
not even one step downstream of that. People&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:03.480 --> 01:12:08.820
have been excited about the possibility that&nbsp;
changes in biochemical signaling at the level&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:08.820 --> 01:12:14.160
of beta-arrestin might be the next step after the&nbsp;
receptor that might be common. We don't see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:14.160 --> 01:12:19.860
to be the case in our studies. And what it seems&nbsp;
to be is downstream of that even more, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:20.820 --> 01:12:25.860
regulation of the extracellular&nbsp;
matrix, which we think is sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:25.860 --> 01:12:30.600
the final common pathway that happens.
And the working model that we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:31.380 --> 01:12:40.680
is that psychedelics bind to their receptor,&nbsp;
and when they sit in that receptor for too long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:41.280 --> 01:12:46.500
it signals to the cell, "Oh, no.&nbsp;
Oh, no. Excitotoxicity is coming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:47.400 --> 01:12:53.880
Hit the reset button." And what that does through&nbsp;
a variety of different mechanisms is we think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:53.880 --> 01:12:59.280
and we don't have strong evidence to support&nbsp;
this, but this is our working model, is that that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:59.280 --> 01:13:06.480
hit the reset button, involves calling in all&nbsp;
of the receptors, internalizing them, cleaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:06.480 --> 01:13:12.480
up the synapse, degrading the extracellular&nbsp;
matrix that is holding the synapse together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:12.480 --> 01:13:23.100
And the last step of making a synapse mature&nbsp;
and stable. And that hard reset is enabling the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:23.100 --> 01:13:29.160
reintegration of those baby receptors that are&nbsp;
more sensitive and able to induce plasticity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:29.160 --> 01:13:34.200
That's sort of our working model.
Tim Ferriss: So I'm curious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:34.920 --> 01:13:41.100
just to revisit kappa-opioid for a second, because&nbsp;
the only kappa-opioid agonist that I'm familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:41.100 --> 01:13:48.240
with is Salvinorin A. Some people may know&nbsp;
Salvia divinorum, which from a phenomenological&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:48.240 --> 01:13:53.940
perspective, to get real fancy, subjectively&nbsp;
people find it typically pretty dysphoric. And I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:53.940 --> 01:14:02.220
just wondering how, perhaps it's not Salvinorin&nbsp;
A, but any kappa-opioid agonist might be used&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:02.220 --> 01:14:06.060
therapeutically, what applications might exist?
Dr. Gül Dölen: Yeah, I mean, people have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:06.060 --> 01:14:11.460
excited about kappa-opioids because&nbsp;
it's thought to be like, that's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:11.460 --> 01:14:15.840
to be the one that's going to make you not be&nbsp;
addicted to it. And it's not very pleasurable.&nbsp;

01:14:15.840 --> 01:14:20.040
Tim Ferriss: That's for sure.
Dr. Gül Dölen: It's not very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:20.040 --> 01:14:24.960
pleasurable. Nobody wants to take it. I&nbsp;
don't want to overstep the kappa-opioid&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:24.960 --> 01:14:32.100
stuff. The reason that we got interested in it&nbsp;
is because the ibogaine also has high affinity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:32.100 --> 01:14:38.220
for kappa-opioid receptors, but it also accesses&nbsp;
serotonin transporters. So I really don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:38.220 --> 01:14:43.080
what the answer is there. I thought the&nbsp;
beta-arrestin story was going to nicely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:43.080 --> 01:14:47.400
sew everything up and it just didn't.
Tim Ferriss: So what is beta-arrestin? I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:47.400 --> 01:14:51.960
to plead ignorance. I don't know what that is.
Dr. Gül Dölen: Basically most of the psychedelic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:51.960 --> 01:14:59.820
drugs, except for ketamine and PCP, are acting at&nbsp;
what are called G-protein coupled receptors. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:59.820 --> 01:15:06.900
when I was talking about NMDA receptors versus&nbsp;
mGluR receptors, the difference is mGluRs are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:06.900 --> 01:15:12.660
G-protein coupled receptors. And NMDA receptors&nbsp;
are ion channels, so they're inotropic receptors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:12.660 --> 01:15:18.780
So metabotropic inotropic, it has to do with how&nbsp;
they're coupled to internal signaling. So all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:18.780 --> 01:15:27.660
the G-protein coupled receptors, including almost&nbsp;
all of the serotonin receptors, they work by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:27.660 --> 01:15:34.980
when the drugs binds to their binding pocket,&nbsp;
they induce a series of biochemical events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:34.980 --> 01:15:43.500
either excitatory or inhibitory, biochemical&nbsp;
changes downstream that are sort of the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:43.500 --> 01:15:50.400
messengers, if you will, of the receptor binding.
Now that's just how they normally signal. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:50.400 --> 01:16:00.120
when a drug like LSD binds to the serotonin 2A&nbsp;
receptor, Brian Roth's group has shown that when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:00.120 --> 01:16:07.080
it sits in its receptor, it has an unusually&nbsp;
long off rate. So normally when transmitters&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:07.080 --> 01:16:10.560
get in their receptor, they jump in and&nbsp;
they jump right back out. So they pop in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:10.560 --> 01:16:17.280
pop out really, really fast, less than a second&nbsp;
time scale. LSD, when it sits in the serotonin&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:17.280 --> 01:16:24.000
2A receptor binding pocket, a little lid comes&nbsp;
down and locks it into place. And when that lid&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:24.000 --> 01:16:31.602
locks the LSD into place, it's off rate goes to&nbsp;
four and a half hours, which is flicking insane.&nbsp;

01:16:31.602 --> 01:16:34.320
Tim Ferriss: It's going to be a long night.
Dr. Gül Dölen: It's going to be a long night.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:34.320 --> 01:16:42.300
Exactly. And that's the half off. So only half of&nbsp;
it comes off in four and a half hours. Right? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:42.300 --> 01:16:48.960
so this is a really important insight into&nbsp;
why it is that LSDs effects last so long.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:48.960 --> 01:16:55.980
But when it sits in that receptor for too long,&nbsp;
instead of signaling through the normal G-protein&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:55.980 --> 01:17:01.860
coupled signaling pathway, what happens is&nbsp;
it starts sending signal to the cell. "No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:01.860 --> 01:17:07.320
instead of G-protein, you need to hit a&nbsp;
different biochemical pathway. We need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:07.320 --> 01:17:13.320
trigger beta-arrestin." And beta-arrestin is just&nbsp;
another biochemical pathway that gets triggered by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:13.320 --> 01:17:19.920
that receptor being activated too long. And what&nbsp;
is thought that beta-arrestin does is rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:19.920 --> 01:17:26.220
do the second messenger signaling, it says to&nbsp;
the cell, "Pull that receptor out, we're about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:26.220 --> 01:17:30.720
to get toxic damage from too much receptor&nbsp;
activation." So beta-arrestin and signaling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:30.720 --> 01:17:36.780
historically has been thought to be a signal&nbsp;
to the cell that says, "Pull in the receptors."&nbsp;

01:17:37.320 --> 01:17:45.360
And so I really loved the idea that even though&nbsp;
not all psychedelics bind to the serotonin 2A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:45.360 --> 01:17:52.920
most of them, you could imagine a scenario where&nbsp;
they were triggering beta-arrestin signaling over&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:52.920 --> 01:17:58.680
G-protein coupled signaling, and that if&nbsp;
you knocked out the beta-arrestin protein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:58.680 --> 01:18:04.740
that you could block the ability of psychedelics&nbsp;
to reopen critical periods. And while that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:04.740 --> 01:18:10.320
true for some of the psychedelics, it turned&nbsp;
out not to be the universal mechanism that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:10.320 --> 01:18:14.580
spans across all of the psychedelics. So that&nbsp;
was a little bit of a bummer, I have to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:14.580 --> 01:18:19.500
because I thought, "All right, we'll solve&nbsp;
it. It'll be done, and I can rest easy."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:19.500 --> 01:18:24.480
But it didn't work out that simple. Mother&nbsp;
nature always likes to trip us up like that.&nbsp;

01:18:24.480 --> 01:18:28.680
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, the mother nature curve&nbsp;
ball seems to come more often than not.&nbsp;

01:18:29.220 --> 01:18:36.300
I do want to get to strokes and how they fit in.&nbsp;
But before we get there, I have to scratch an itch&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:36.300 --> 01:18:41.100
because I've been wanting to ask you for a while&nbsp;
now. Excuse me. First, a bit of background. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:41.100 --> 01:18:46.500
mentioned Alexander Shulgin, also known as Sasha&nbsp;
Shulgin. For people who don't recognize the name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:48.120 --> 01:18:56.220
incredibly adept, incredibly prolific biochemist,&nbsp;
very much recommend people check out PiHKAL&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:56.220 --> 01:19:05.820
and TiHKAL, which he wrote with his wife.
But the contrast you drew earlier was one of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:05.820 --> 01:19:11.460
say, maybe human centricity. So he not only&nbsp;
created or resurrected hundreds, maybe thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:11.460 --> 01:19:18.720
of different psychedelic compounds, but also was&nbsp;
a very dedicated self-experimenter. The tasting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:18.720 --> 01:19:25.620
parties were very famous, of course. Where&nbsp;
do you most strongly perhaps agree or align&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:25.620 --> 01:19:31.920
with Sasha? What are things that you perhaps&nbsp;
have in common, if anything comes to mind?&nbsp;

01:19:31.920 --> 01:19:37.440
Dr. Gül Dölen: Yeah, I've got to tell you a funny&nbsp;
story of when he came to visit MIT when I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:37.440 --> 01:19:45.120
a graduate student. It was the opening party&nbsp;
for the new building. I won't name any names,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:45.120 --> 01:19:52.140
but everybody had invited a bunch of Nobel&nbsp;
Prize winners who had discovered every cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:52.140 --> 01:19:58.500
thing in biology. They all got up there and not&nbsp;
necessarily knew anything about neuroscience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:58.500 --> 01:20:05.100
they were all older, and they gave a&nbsp;
little biography and then sat back down.&nbsp;

01:20:05.100 --> 01:20:12.180
Then Sasha got up on stage. He was just basically&nbsp;
the same age as everybody else, the only person&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:12.180 --> 01:20:19.860
without a Nobel Prize on that stage, and yet&nbsp;
he was so alert and so adept. He was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:19.860 --> 01:20:24.720
"Then we added this methyl group over here and&nbsp;
this happened and the behavioral response. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:24.720 --> 01:20:30.300
we did this and we got ego this, and we put that&nbsp;
on there." All of the neuroscientists were just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:30.300 --> 01:20:37.860
rapt attention because this is it. This is&nbsp;
what we all dream of as neuroscientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:37.860 --> 01:20:46.500
is to have some manipulation that we can give, in&nbsp;
this case a drug, that is dramatically altering&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:46.500 --> 01:20:50.520
the way that the brain is functioning&nbsp;
and interpreting the world around it.&nbsp;

01:20:50.520 --> 01:20:57.240
And here is this chemist explaining to us how&nbsp;
this could be done just by adding a methyl&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:57.240 --> 01:21:02.160
group in this position versus that position. We&nbsp;
were all just blown away. I don't think there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:02.160 --> 01:21:08.160
was anybody who walked away from that lecture&nbsp;
thinking, gosh, psychedelics should just stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:08.160 --> 01:21:12.960
in the backwaters, we shouldn't care about&nbsp;
this as neuroscience. Obviously not. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:12.960 --> 01:21:18.360
it's how I got excited about neuroscience&nbsp;
to begin with. I took this class when I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:18.360 --> 01:21:24.300
an undergrad called Drugs, Brain, and Behavior.
In that class, the first day I saw the photograph&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:24.300 --> 01:21:31.380
of the molecule of LSD sitting right next to the&nbsp;
molecule of serotonin and the similarities between&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:31.380 --> 01:21:36.660
them, I was like, "This is it. This is how we're&nbsp;
going to crack it. This is how we're going to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:36.660 --> 01:21:43.560
at those hard questions of neuroscience because&nbsp;
here are chemicals that can alter our entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:43.560 --> 01:21:50.340
sense of reality, consciousness, perception,&nbsp;
time, self, space, everything." And I am not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:50.340 --> 01:21:56.520
alone. I think most neuroscientists who have tried&nbsp;
psychedelics would have exactly the same response.&nbsp;

01:21:57.420 --> 01:22:05.160
The difference between me and Sasha really in&nbsp;
terms of our approach is that Sasha comes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:05.160 --> 01:22:14.460
an older tradition in science, which very much&nbsp;
prioritized the do the experiment on yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:14.460 --> 01:22:21.360
idea. That has a long and glorious tradition&nbsp;
in science. For example, we have a cure for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:21.900 --> 01:22:28.380
ulcers because there was a biologist who was&nbsp;
convinced that those were caused by a bacteria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:28.380 --> 01:22:35.220
but nobody believed him. So he drank a vial of&nbsp;
bacteria, gave himself ulcers, and then took a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:35.220 --> 01:22:39.840
bunch of antibiotics and cured himself of the&nbsp;
ulcers, and he won the Nobel Prize for that.&nbsp;

01:22:39.840 --> 01:22:46.200
The difference is, with psychedelics, is&nbsp;
that that's a little bit trickier to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:46.200 --> 01:22:54.540
because psychedelics have this property, which&nbsp;
William James pointed out over 100 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:54.540 --> 01:23:03.060
of creating this sense of — what he called the&nbsp;
noetic property, this feeling that "Now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:03.060 --> 01:23:08.520
I've had this experience, I know the really&nbsp;
real. The true truth has been revealed to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:08.520 --> 01:23:14.640
and everything before this moment was just a&nbsp;
facade or some lesser truth or some limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:15.300 --> 01:23:23.100
access to the truth. But now I really know."&nbsp;
For a scientist, that's pretty dangerous.&nbsp;

01:23:23.100 --> 01:23:30.000
For a scientist, you always want to be able to&nbsp;
have a little bit of skepticism about what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:37.740
is that you have discovered; you always want&nbsp;
to keep your ideas as provisional until the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:37.740 --> 01:23:43.980
next test comes along. It should always&nbsp;
be a working model, never "This is the 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:43.980 --> 01:23:49.920
commandments of neuroscience." That's not the&nbsp;
way that we do science. So the noetic property,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:49.920 --> 01:23:58.740
I think, does mean that that experiment where&nbsp;
you take a bunch of bacteria and give yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:58.740 --> 01:24:05.420
ulcers is a little bit harder to justify&nbsp;
in this neuroscience now. Let's not lie.&nbsp;

01:24:05.420 --> 01:24:09.000
Tim Ferriss: In psychedelics. Yeah.&nbsp;
Hard to be an objective or strive&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:09.000 --> 01:24:13.440
to be an objective scientist if you turn&nbsp;
yourself into David Koresh accidentally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:17.700 --> 01:24:24.060
So how do strokes fit into this?
Dr. Gül Dölen: For a long time now, clinically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:24.060 --> 01:24:31.500
we have known that right after you have a&nbsp;
stroke, you have this very short window of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:33.540 --> 01:24:38.760
really, really closed by three months, but really&nbsp;
you want to get in there within six weeks to two&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:38.760 --> 01:24:46.260
months or so, where physical therapy is going to&nbsp;
have its maximal effect or benefit for helping you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:46.260 --> 01:24:52.680
to recover the motor function that you lost&nbsp;
because of the stroke. That window of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:53.640 --> 01:24:59.760
we don't exactly know why it closes.
But it seems to be that it's not that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:59.760 --> 01:25:07.020
the damaged part of the brain suddenly resprouts&nbsp;
neurons, it's that the brain regions immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:07.020 --> 01:25:17.700
adjacent to the damaged part of the brain become&nbsp;
flexible again and able to perform or control the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:17.700 --> 01:25:25.920
motor functions that were lost by the brain region&nbsp;
immediately next to it. That window closes just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:25.920 --> 01:25:33.060
like all other critical periods close. So far, the&nbsp;
best way that we have for reopening that critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:33.060 --> 01:25:38.640
period is to give another stroke. But nobody&nbsp;
wants to cure stroke by giving another stroke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:39.240 --> 01:25:43.303
so that's just not a clinically useful way.
Tim Ferriss: Hard to sell. Hard to sell that.&nbsp;

01:25:43.303 --> 01:25:53.760
Dr. Gül Dölen: Yeah, definitely hard&nbsp;
to sell. We like the idea that maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:54.780 --> 01:26:01.620
we could branch out into other critical periods&nbsp;
like this one to see whether or not psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:01.620 --> 01:26:06.900
can reopen that critical period for stroke.&nbsp;
We have a collaboration with Steven Zeiler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:06.900 --> 01:26:12.300
who's a neurologist at Johns Hopkins&nbsp;
who has developed a rodent model for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:12.960 --> 01:26:22.200
that critical period of motor recovery following&nbsp;
stroke. He also has a stroke clinic at Hopkins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:22.800 --> 01:26:30.900
We're working closely with them to see whether&nbsp;
or not this idea really can help these patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:30.900 --> 01:26:38.220
which basically it's a 400,000 patients a year&nbsp;
in the United States who have debilitating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:38.220 --> 01:26:45.000
lasting impairments because of stroke.
It's a huge number of people. I think if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:45.000 --> 01:26:51.060
this ends up being true, I think it's — it's super&nbsp;
exciting because here's something that we started&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:51.060 --> 01:26:57.300
out with, "I'm just curious what MDMA will do&nbsp;
in an octopus." It was totally curiosity-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:57.300 --> 01:27:05.820
science. To see it evolve into something&nbsp;
that can potentially help so many people is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:06.660 --> 01:27:13.020
as a scientist, just super gratifying. It's&nbsp;
pretty rare. Nobody really gets to have those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:13.020 --> 01:27:18.060
kinds of woo-hoo success stories. So that's&nbsp;
very gratifying to me on a personal level.&nbsp;

01:27:18.060 --> 01:27:25.380
But also, I think that for all of the psychedelics&nbsp;
enthusiasts out there, it's a lesson in how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:25.380 --> 01:27:32.820
important it is to never stop learning, never&nbsp;
stop questioning. I think there was an intuition&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:32.820 --> 01:27:38.880
a few years ago that, well, indigenous people&nbsp;
have been using psychedelics forever, we don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:38.880 --> 01:27:44.340
really need to understand their mechanisms, we&nbsp;
don't really need to dig into how it is these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:44.340 --> 01:27:49.140
things work because we know they're going to work&nbsp;
because they've got this long history behind them.&nbsp;

01:27:49.140 --> 01:27:54.360
But I think that if this ends up being&nbsp;
true, that this mechanistic explanation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:54.360 --> 01:28:00.180
can really open up whole new avenues that&nbsp;
people hadn't been thinking of before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:01.140 --> 01:28:06.660
then I think that that's a testament to&nbsp;
the importance of always keep an open mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:06.660 --> 01:28:10.680
always look for more answers, more&nbsp;
questions, and keep searching.&nbsp;

01:28:10.680 --> 01:28:18.000
Tim Ferriss: Absolutely. I would also say, even&nbsp;
if, let's just say hypothetically, these various&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:18.000 --> 01:28:23.460
indigenous cultures have figured it out, let's&nbsp;
just take that as an assumption for a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:23.460 --> 01:28:28.020
they've figured out how to use these things, but&nbsp;
the scale at which they're used and the context&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:28.020 --> 01:28:35.400
within which they're used are on the order&nbsp;
of, say, max 100 or several hundred people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:35.400 --> 01:28:43.140
within the safety net of certain rituals&nbsp;
and familial and social support structures&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:43.140 --> 01:28:49.260
that it cannot be replicated at scale in say a&nbsp;
western treatment paradigm. You need new tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:50.040 --> 01:28:57.420
and that requires exploration and testing.
Dr. Gül Dölen: Let me just riff on that for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:57.420 --> 01:29:04.080
a second because I think that that's a really&nbsp;
important point about the context. The context&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:04.080 --> 01:29:12.300
of disease is also different in this culture&nbsp;
versus other cultures. How a disease is defined&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:12.300 --> 01:29:17.700
is different across cultures and how you might&nbsp;
approach a disease is defined differently across&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:17.700 --> 01:29:25.860
cultures. A good example is the way that ibogaine&nbsp;
is used traditionally. These cultures using&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:25.860 --> 01:29:32.340
ibogaine for traditional healing practices, it's&nbsp;
not just that the person taking it has a disease&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:32.340 --> 01:29:38.400
and they're being treated. It's a mix between&nbsp;
they have a disease but they also have something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:38.400 --> 01:29:46.500
to share with the rest of their community.
The whole ibogaine experience is encapsulated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:46.500 --> 01:29:53.040
in a bunch of things that we as Western&nbsp;
doctors might dismiss as just ritual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:53.040 --> 01:30:00.720
but include things like face painting and social&nbsp;
touch and the whole community is involved for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:00.720 --> 01:30:08.460
three-day long trip that the person goes from one&nbsp;
house to another. It turns out that a lot of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:08.460 --> 01:30:15.540
elements that we might not necessarily recognize&nbsp;
as important, that we might dismiss as ritual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:16.440 --> 01:30:23.280
in a mouse, we can recapitulate. We know&nbsp;
that MDMA's ability to reopen the social&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:23.280 --> 01:30:29.220
critical period is social-context dependent. If&nbsp;
we give it in isolation, then it doesn't reopen.&nbsp;

01:30:29.760 --> 01:30:35.400
It turns out that there are all of these elements&nbsp;
that go beyond just the molecule and we can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:35.400 --> 01:30:40.500
just import, take the thing that we decide&nbsp;
is the most important element, the molecule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:40.500 --> 01:30:47.460
and import it into our medicinal practices and&nbsp;
our cultural history without understanding it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:47.460 --> 01:30:53.460
because many of those elements of the ritual&nbsp;
might be actually really important to how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:53.460 --> 01:30:58.440
these drugs are working therapeutically.&nbsp;
Unless we understand it from our context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:58.440 --> 01:31:04.680
we won't be able to readily adapt them, I think.
Tim Ferriss: 100 percent. To maybe clarify the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:04.680 --> 01:31:11.340
point I was trying to make somewhat inelegantly,&nbsp;
I think all of these practices are worth studying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:11.340 --> 01:31:15.420
especially the things that have had some degree&nbsp;
of longevity and persisted over time that seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:15.420 --> 01:31:21.180
be adaptive or could potentially, plausibly&nbsp;
be helpful in the therapeutic effects. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:21.900 --> 01:31:25.860
I push back, maybe it's because I'm in&nbsp;
Austin and you can't throw a rock without&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:25.860 --> 01:31:31.080
meeting someone who did a weekend online&nbsp;
course and is now a self-proclaimed shaman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:31.080 --> 01:31:36.000
so I have a certain sensitivity to it.
But this romanticized, I think very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:36.000 --> 01:31:41.340
naive notion that you can just copy and paste&nbsp;
things whole cloth from one area to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:42.360 --> 01:31:49.860
is not just naive but also quite dangerous.&nbsp;
And I appreciate you saying what you just said.&nbsp;

01:31:49.860 --> 01:31:55.980
If you flash forward in your mind,&nbsp;
I recognize there's some imagination&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:55.980 --> 01:32:03.060
and speculation required here, but if this&nbsp;
critical period work and maybe the offshoots&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:03.060 --> 01:32:10.560
of that work continue to gain support&nbsp;
through rigorous science and evidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:11.280 --> 01:32:19.860
what might therapeutic interventions or&nbsp;
treatments look like 10 years from now?&nbsp;

01:32:19.860 --> 01:32:25.080
I'm pulling that number out of nowhere. But&nbsp;
I'm curious to know in your mind's eye what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:25.080 --> 01:32:30.600
you think things could look like if this&nbsp;
continues to develop in the direction that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:31.800 --> 01:32:37.800
at least now you're pursuing research.
Dr. Gül Dölen: Yeah. I think for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:37.800 --> 01:32:45.540
if this ends up being true, then it's going to be&nbsp;
a pretty big paradigm shift for neuropsychiatric&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:45.540 --> 01:32:52.860
treatments because — let me just back up and&nbsp;
say I have to give a little bit of credit to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:52.860 --> 01:33:00.420
MAPS here because MAPS did a lot of really hard&nbsp;
work to convince the FDA that the way to design&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:00.420 --> 01:33:09.480
clinical trials around psychedelics was to abandon&nbsp;
the one-variable-at-a-time test model and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:09.480 --> 01:33:16.740
"Look, we really need to pair these drugs&nbsp;
with psychotherapy because the drugs are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:16.740 --> 01:33:22.140
not doing the magic by themselves. It's&nbsp;
really this combination that's working."&nbsp;

01:33:23.820 --> 01:33:32.100
That I think was a big shift in the way that the&nbsp;
FDA wants us to test drugs in clinical trials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:32.820 --> 01:33:38.880
I think that we're beginning to see more and more&nbsp;
evidence that that context is really what matters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:39.600 --> 01:33:48.060
For example, the clinical trials where context is&nbsp;
secondary, where the psychotherapy is essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:49.500 --> 01:33:56.880
there in case somebody has a bad trip, but it's&nbsp;
not really front and center to the intervention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:56.880 --> 01:34:03.300
then I think those trials for psilocybin, for&nbsp;
example, aren't working as well as the trials&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:03.300 --> 01:34:10.920
where it's really seen as an adjunct to the&nbsp;
primary therapy, which is the psychotherapy.&nbsp;

01:34:10.920 --> 01:34:15.840
Now, if we're right and the critical&nbsp;
period reopening explanation is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:16.560 --> 01:34:23.160
shift in the framework for how we understand these&nbsp;
therapeutic effects, then in 10 years from now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:23.160 --> 01:34:30.660
the way that psychedelics are going to be used is&nbsp;
going to be trying to identify that right context&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:30.660 --> 01:34:36.900
for the right disease. So while an inter-directed&nbsp;
trip with a lot of psychotherapy makes a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:36.900 --> 01:34:43.320
sense for PTSD and addiction and depression,&nbsp;
it's probably the wrong context for stroke.&nbsp;

01:34:43.920 --> 01:34:51.420
Whereas doing a lot of physical therapy and&nbsp;
movement and playing virtual reality motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:51.420 --> 01:34:59.220
games might be the right context for giving the&nbsp;
psychedelic for stroke, for allergy maybe the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:59.220 --> 01:35:03.840
right context is snuggling up to that horse or&nbsp;
that dog or that cat that you're allergic to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:04.680 --> 01:35:13.500
So I think the future, the therapeutic potential&nbsp;
for this is going to be — the opportunity is huge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:13.500 --> 01:35:20.940
but the devil is going to be in the details&nbsp;
in terms of identifying the right context and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:20.940 --> 01:35:28.140
figuring out how to get activation of the right&nbsp;
neural circuit that's relevant for the brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:28.140 --> 01:35:32.700
transformation that you want to see happen.
Tim Ferriss: If we take the case of stroke&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:32.700 --> 01:35:37.860
patients, for instance, if we just assume for&nbsp;
the moment that we're using human subjects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:38.940 --> 01:35:46.860
how might you think about currently what&nbsp;
type of experimental design you would use&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:46.860 --> 01:35:53.040
with respect to dosing? Because if&nbsp;
you have this less inter-directed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:54.000 --> 01:36:01.080
say, higher dose experience with a potential&nbsp;
for these noetic qualities that you described,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:01.080 --> 01:36:07.320
but rather you're following instructions, you are&nbsp;
practicing skills using, potentially, VR, do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:07.320 --> 01:36:13.860
have any thoughts on how you design the study or&nbsp;
what the minimum effective dose might look like?&nbsp;

01:36:14.580 --> 01:36:17.160
Dr. Gül Dölen: Yeah. There are two&nbsp;
questions in there, let me just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:18.780 --> 01:36:23.220
pull them apart for a second. The dose issue,&nbsp;
our studies that we've done so far suggest that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:23.220 --> 01:36:28.380
there's a pretty narrow dose response for&nbsp;
reopening critical periods and that matches&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:28.380 --> 01:36:34.440
what we see in humans for the acute subjective&nbsp;
effects. But we don't see any evidence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:34.980 --> 01:36:42.360
increasing the dose necessarily increases the&nbsp;
opening of the critical period or how long the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:42.360 --> 01:36:48.060
duration of the critical period open state.
Basically, what we find is that if the acute&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:48.060 --> 01:36:53.640
subjective effects are short, like with ketamine,&nbsp;
then the window only stays open for a very short&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:53.640 --> 01:36:57.180
amount of time, less than a week. Whereas&nbsp;
if the window stays open for — if the acute&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:57.180 --> 01:37:02.700
subjective effects lasts a really long time,&nbsp;
like with ibogaine which lasts 36 to 72 hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:02.700 --> 01:37:08.460
then the window, the critical period&nbsp;
window, stays open for much longer. In mice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:08.460 --> 01:37:13.560
that's four weeks. In humans, that may translate&nbsp;
to four weeks or four months. We're not sure.&nbsp;

01:37:16.080 --> 01:37:21.600
And then psilocybin, MDMA, and LSD are in the&nbsp;
middle there between those two extremes. But it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:21.600 --> 01:37:27.960
not the case that if you double the dose of LSD,&nbsp;
then you can get LSD to open the critical period&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:27.960 --> 01:37:34.620
longer, for example. So that dosing business, I&nbsp;
think, is going to be a little bit of a heartbreak&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:34.620 --> 01:37:41.100
for all of the pharmaceutical companies out&nbsp;
there that are essentially trying to engineer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:41.100 --> 01:37:47.760
out the psychedelic effects by shortening&nbsp;
the duration of the psychedelic effects to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:48.480 --> 01:37:55.020
get less side effects and a shorter journey that&nbsp;
will be easier to manage in a clinical setting.&nbsp;

01:37:55.020 --> 01:38:00.660
Again, what can you do? Nature is nature. But&nbsp;
I do think that it changes the model of how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:00.660 --> 01:38:06.660
approach these. Rather than thinking of these as&nbsp;
next generation anxiolytics or next generation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:06.660 --> 01:38:12.960
anti-depressants or SSRIs, what we really need&nbsp;
to be thinking of these as is more like surgery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:14.580 --> 01:38:19.020
If you have a heart attack, you go and you have&nbsp;
open heart surgery, it lasts for eight hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:19.740 --> 01:38:25.440
You have maybe two or three weeks afterwards&nbsp;
where you are required to be on bedrest and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:25.440 --> 01:38:32.760
take things easy. But then you're cured.
So who wouldn't rather be cured of a heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:32.760 --> 01:38:37.920
attack than have to be on a medicine for the&nbsp;
rest of their lives? I think most people see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:37.920 --> 01:38:43.800
that as the major promise of psychedelics. So&nbsp;
in terms of the therapeutic approach, I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:44.340 --> 01:38:51.240
the way I'm framing this stroke trial is really&nbsp;
I'm not worried about the fact that they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:51.240 --> 01:38:57.000
going to have to do a psychedelic. Potentially,&nbsp;
the maximum benefit is going to be from these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:57.000 --> 01:39:02.640
long-acting psychedelics, because I think that&nbsp;
these motor critical periods are pretty strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:02.640 --> 01:39:10.380
closes and so you want to reopen them for as long&nbsp;
as you possibly can to get the maximal effect.&nbsp;

01:39:11.160 --> 01:39:16.140
We'll have to work out how to do them back&nbsp;
to back to back. But that's how I'm thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:16.140 --> 01:39:22.860
about it there. The second thing I want&nbsp;
to unpack about your question is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:24.120 --> 01:39:29.940
you mentioned for the stroke that you were&nbsp;
imagining that we would be giving the psychedelic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:29.940 --> 01:39:36.480
and the person would be practicing a bunch of&nbsp;
things in a very specific, directed way. Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:36.480 --> 01:39:40.260
that's not how I'm imagining that this will work.
Tim Ferriss: I got it. It would be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:40.260 --> 01:39:42.660
sequential, not concurrent. It would —
Dr. Gül Dölen: No, no, no. It would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:42.660 --> 01:39:48.420
concurrent, but I would say it's not going to be&nbsp;
practice-based. One of the things that I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:48.420 --> 01:39:56.400
want to emphasize is part of the reason that I&nbsp;
paired up with Steve Zeiler and John Krakauer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:56.400 --> 01:40:06.840
at Hopkins is that their approach for treating&nbsp;
stroke during the normal one, the critical period&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:06.840 --> 01:40:15.600
is already open, is that they've said, "Well, most&nbsp;
of the physical therapy that patients receive is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:15.600 --> 01:40:23.700
very goal directed. I need to learn how to zip my&nbsp;
pants with the other hand, I need to learn how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:23.700 --> 01:40:29.400
comb my hair with the other hand."
So it's very much practical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:31.140 --> 01:40:39.300
solving the problem another way, whereas the way&nbsp;
that we learn those motor patterns when we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:39.300 --> 01:40:48.240
children is much more goal undirected to kind&nbsp;
of play, wandering, don't care if I fall down&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:48.240 --> 01:40:56.220
a million times. I mean, there's this great idea&nbsp;
that children, when they're learning how to walk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:56.220 --> 01:41:03.780
will walk the equivalent of seven football fields&nbsp;
in an hour. Now the only time I've ever seen an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:03.780 --> 01:41:09.960
adult do that is on MDMA. They will just dance&nbsp;
and they don't care if they look like fools.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:09.960 --> 01:41:14.940
They don't care if they're not good dancers.
It's not goal-directed. They're just enjoying&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:14.940 --> 01:41:24.420
the play of doing the motion. What I like about&nbsp;
Steve and John's approach to motor rehabilitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:25.200 --> 01:41:30.840
without psychedelics is that their virtual&nbsp;
reality system that they've built is very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:30.840 --> 01:41:37.380
capitalizing on this non-goal directed, play-like&nbsp;
behavior. And I think that's going to be the key.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:37.980 --> 01:41:45.060
So I think if we can get people on psychedelics&nbsp;
plus put them in this motor play context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:45.060 --> 01:41:51.420
then we can maximally recapitulate the&nbsp;
conditions that we learn how to do motor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:51.420 --> 01:41:59.220
function when we're developing as children.
Tim Ferriss: Speaking of fun, I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:00.060 --> 01:42:09.360
go back in time to Bears Lab at MIT. This is&nbsp;
from Spectrum News, reading a section of this.&nbsp;

01:42:09.360 --> 01:42:14.580
"He had stressed the importance of science&nbsp;
being fun," and the quote is, "'If you lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:14.580 --> 01:42:18.960
sight of the fun in science, it's hardly worth&nbsp;
continuing,' he used to say. Dölen decided that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:18.960 --> 01:42:22.860
if she was indeed going to give up a life of&nbsp;
science, she would go out on her own terms."&nbsp;

01:42:23.520 --> 01:42:29.460
Could you unpack that last sentence?&nbsp;
Were you considering giving up a life&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:29.460 --> 01:42:38.700
of science? Then how did that lead to&nbsp;
octopuses? Because it seems like that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:39.720 --> 01:42:48.000
focus then opened up Pandora's box in the&nbsp;
most positive way. I want to go back and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:50.340 --> 01:42:56.940
do a little bit of postmortem on what exactly&nbsp;
happened. So please, if you wouldn't mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:56.940 --> 01:43:01.620
just tell the story of that period.
Dr. Gül Dölen: When you start your own lab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:01.620 --> 01:43:07.860
you get a bunch of money. That startup&nbsp;
package is great to get you started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:07.860 --> 01:43:14.400
hire some people buy some equipment, get your&nbsp;
lab established without having to worry too&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:14.400 --> 01:43:19.800
much about the funding. When I started my lab,&nbsp;
I got a nice startup package. I immediately got&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:19.800 --> 01:43:26.460
a couple of private foundation grants which&nbsp;
were very helpful to help me establish the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:26.460 --> 01:43:35.340
lab. But you really can't, in academia, survive&nbsp;
unless you can also get funding from the NIH.&nbsp;

01:43:36.120 --> 01:43:43.740
These big NIH grants, in my career, in my&nbsp;
lifetime, have gotten harder and harder to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:43.740 --> 01:43:50.520
get. It used to be that people who were young&nbsp;
and had a lot of creative ideas had much higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:50.520 --> 01:43:56.576
success rates than people who were older and just&nbsp;
redoing the same experiments with the different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:56.576 --> 01:44:02.880
— whatever, very conservative rinse and repeat&nbsp;
type of experiments. Over my scientific career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:02.880 --> 01:44:10.920
I have seen that situation essentially reverse. So&nbsp;
people over the age of 65 are getting most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:10.920 --> 01:44:17.580
NIH grants, junior people are really struggling.
There have been some attempts to correct that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:17.580 --> 01:44:27.120
but they just haven't worked. It's just incredibly&nbsp;
difficult to get funding. Then if you add on top&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:27.120 --> 01:44:33.660
of that, the funding has gotten so conservative&nbsp;
that basically the way that I write NIH grants&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:33.660 --> 01:44:40.080
right now is — once I have all of the results that&nbsp;
are going to make a cool Nature paper, I write&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:40.080 --> 01:44:47.160
a grant on all of the boring experiments that I&nbsp;
didn't do it for that paper but that I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:48.600 --> 01:44:52.920
the reviewers might ask me for, and I package&nbsp;
that together and I send that to the NIH.&nbsp;

01:44:52.920 --> 01:44:59.280
That's how conservative my grants really are&nbsp;
when I applied to NIH funding. And that's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:59.280 --> 01:45:07.260
successful. But there was a moment a few months&nbsp;
before the octopus paper, before we even did that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:07.260 --> 01:45:15.300
experiment, where I was just beginning to feel&nbsp;
like I'm not going to make it. I applied to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:15.300 --> 01:45:19.920
NIH 17 times before I got my first NIH grant.
Tim Ferriss: Wow!&nbsp;

01:45:19.920 --> 01:45:25.500
Dr. Gül Dölen: I think part of it was because Iwas&nbsp;
proposing these crazy ideas that people were like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:25.500 --> 01:45:31.560
"There's no critical period for social behavior."&nbsp;
What? "Nobody's ever going to use psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:31.560 --> 01:45:37.200
clinically." What? "No, that'll never work.&nbsp;
Oh, you made those mice — too bad you didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:37.200 --> 01:45:42.180
use them for the experiment that I would've&nbsp;
used them for. You're just a junior person.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:42.180 --> 01:45:47.580
You have no established track record. No, no,&nbsp;
no, no, no, no, no, no." I was feeling really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:48.180 --> 01:45:54.240
demoralized and I was like, "I can't do this."
I thought I was becoming a scientist because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:54.240 --> 01:46:01.980
was going to get to pursue these big questions and&nbsp;
be creative and steer my own ship. That's what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:01.980 --> 01:46:08.400
had worked so hard for as a graduate student,&nbsp;
as a postdoc, as a medical student. That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:08.400 --> 01:46:19.740
the dream that I was sold. I was pretty devastated&nbsp;
by how many rejections I got. In that moment when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:19.740 --> 01:46:24.591
I was like, "All right, I'm going to try a couple&nbsp;
more times, but if I don't get it I'm out as soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:24.591 --> 01:46:27.780
as I…" My department was very supportive. They&nbsp;
were like, "You'll get there, you'll get there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:27.780 --> 01:46:31.500
you'll get there. We'll cover you. Don't&nbsp;
worry, don't fire anybody. You're good."&nbsp;

01:46:31.500 --> 01:46:39.000
But still, it's a blow to your ego if you can't&nbsp;
convince your peers at the NIH to give you that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:39.000 --> 01:46:45.600
money. So going out on my own terms, I was just&nbsp;
like, "Look, one of the creative — I've always&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:45.600 --> 01:46:51.900
wanted to do study evolution. I've always&nbsp;
wanted to do something to do with marine&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:51.900 --> 01:47:00.180
neuroscience. I have this opportunity to look at&nbsp;
what happens to this behavior in an octopus. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:00.180 --> 01:47:08.040
just going to go for it and it'll be fun."
Even if it doesn't work, even if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:09.540 --> 01:47:16.680
is not useful for my career in any way at all&nbsp;
— I mean, I would go to these meetings with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:16.680 --> 01:47:21.120
faculty meetings and meetings with my department&nbsp;
chair, and I'd tell him we were planning to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:21.120 --> 01:47:26.220
this octopus stuff, and he'd be like, "Gül, what&nbsp;
are you doing? We hired you to cure autism. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:26.220 --> 01:47:33.060
is this octopus stuff? What?" And I'd just be&nbsp;
like, "Look, you've got to back off. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:33.060 --> 01:47:40.080
what I want to do. This is what's going to make&nbsp;
me feel like science is fun. I'm doing it not in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:40.080 --> 01:47:46.260
a goal-directed way, in a playful, just out of&nbsp;
curiosity. I just want to know what happens."&nbsp;

01:47:48.780 --> 01:47:54.840
Then it worked and it revived my sense of&nbsp;
play and joy and feeling like you can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:54.840 --> 01:48:00.060
those crazy experiments that everybody&nbsp;
thinks are crazy and have fun with it.&nbsp;

01:48:00.060 --> 01:48:05.820
Tim Ferriss: So you, I wouldn't say bet&nbsp;
the farm, but you were willing to go out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:05.820 --> 01:48:14.760
in a blaze of glory and meaning just focusing on&nbsp;
fun and trying to break new ground. It worked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:15.720 --> 01:48:27.600
How do you think about, if you do, preserving&nbsp;
that type of willingness? Maybe the octopus work&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:28.440 --> 01:48:34.140
bought you a golden ticket to now continuing&nbsp;
to do work that is not rinse and repeat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:34.140 --> 01:48:39.600
but perhaps — I'm just spinning a story here.&nbsp;
But I can imagine as you get more established,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:39.600 --> 01:48:45.240
maybe you feel like you have more to protect, so&nbsp;
maybe there's an inclination to take fewer risks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:45.240 --> 01:48:53.340
How do you think about risk taking moving forward?
Dr. Gül Dölen: Yeah. First of all, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:53.340 --> 01:48:57.480
don't want to oversell how big of a risk it was&nbsp;
for me to do that experiment. We were already&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:57.480 --> 01:49:06.420
working on MDMA, we already had reasonable&nbsp;
response from nature about the MDMA critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:06.420 --> 01:49:13.500
period experiment. The octopus thing was really&nbsp;
kind of like — well, we had the MDMA around, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:13.500 --> 01:49:19.380
I've always been curious and I just wanted to see&nbsp;
what would happen. Then a collaborator was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:19.380 --> 01:49:26.220
"Whoa! We have seven octopuses that we can lend&nbsp;
you for a week." And I was like, "Come on down."&nbsp;

01:49:26.220 --> 01:49:33.960
He flew down, he slept on my couch. We stayed up&nbsp;
until two o'clock in the morning every night just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:33.960 --> 01:49:38.760
doing it, mad scientists, because we had to send&nbsp;
the octopuses back by the end of the week. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:38.760 --> 01:49:48.660
just like this mad scientist fun time. So how do I&nbsp;
preserve that? Well, I guess part of, I think, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:48.660 --> 01:49:56.760
reason that we as scientists are seeing less and&nbsp;
less of that crazy stuff is really the funding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:58.860 --> 01:50:06.060
Just to give a sense of this, I think this is&nbsp;
a really missed point, especially with tech.&nbsp;

01:50:07.740 --> 01:50:12.300
In the '50s and '60s, the NSF,&nbsp;
the National Science Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:12.300 --> 01:50:18.660
and the NIH were funding all kinds of crazy&nbsp;
stuff that they just never thought would ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:18.660 --> 01:50:25.800
be useful or have a goal. They were just&nbsp;
funding it. So when Thomas Brock was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:25.800 --> 01:50:31.380
"There are these weird bacteria that live in&nbsp;
these thermal vents in Yellowstone National Park,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:31.380 --> 01:50:35.160
give me some money to go figure out how&nbsp;
they can survive those temperatures,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:36.180 --> 01:50:40.860
NSF was like, "Yeah, sure, here you go."&nbsp;
If they hadn't given him that money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:40.860 --> 01:50:49.020
the PCR test that we all learned about during the&nbsp;
pandemic would not be possible because that enzyme&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:49.020 --> 01:50:54.720
that is required for that test is made by those&nbsp;
bacteria that live in those weird thermal vets.&nbsp;

01:50:55.380 --> 01:51:04.200
So I think this non-goal directed, curiosity-based&nbsp;
science, it doesn't show any usefulness for 40,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:04.200 --> 01:51:11.700
50 years. It takes that long for something&nbsp;
meaningful to come out of that discovery-based,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:11.700 --> 01:51:19.440
curiosity-based science. And I think that the&nbsp;
way that funding constraints and the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:19.440 --> 01:51:28.380
the tech and — even private foundations are very&nbsp;
goal-oriented. It's like, "Well, how can we sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:28.380 --> 01:51:36.600
this? How can we market this?" And the private&nbsp;
foundations have names like Cure Autism Now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:36.600 --> 01:51:46.080
They're very goal-directed. I think that that&nbsp;
kills the motivation or the impetus to just do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:46.080 --> 01:51:53.280
the weird stuff that's just for fun and curiosity.
I would say every person who becomes a scientist&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:53.940 --> 01:52:01.680
at one point or another had that as their&nbsp;
motivation. The strain of the funding crisis&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:01.680 --> 01:52:09.300
is essentially what has forced people to get&nbsp;
more and more and more conservative. So I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:09.300 --> 01:52:15.480
think I'm unique for wanting to just see what&nbsp;
happens when you give octopuses MDMA. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:15.480 --> 01:52:22.020
I think the reason that everybody responded&nbsp;
to that paper so well worldwide is because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:22.020 --> 01:52:29.220
every single one of us has that curiosity, has&nbsp;
that, "I wonder what would happen?" It resonated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:29.220 --> 01:52:34.740
with people, and I just think it mostly gets&nbsp;
beaten out of us because of funding constraints.&nbsp;

01:52:34.740 --> 01:52:40.380
Tim Ferriss: Is there any solution there? I mean,&nbsp;
if you could wave a magic wand, are there any&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:40.380 --> 01:52:45.960
structural changes that would help to alleviate&nbsp;
that? I'm just wondering if anything comes to mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:45.960 --> 01:52:50.820
aside from just, "Come on, guys. Come on. We need&nbsp;
the exploratory stuff, the weird stuff. Otherwise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:50.820 --> 01:52:56.100
we're not actually investing in the long-term&nbsp;
serendipity that has produced all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:57.900 --> 01:53:03.480
world changing technologies." Aside from&nbsp;
just having drinks with people at NIH or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:03.480 --> 01:53:08.940
NSF or elsewhere and having this conversation,&nbsp;
are there other levers that could be pulled or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:08.940 --> 01:53:11.880
other changes that could be made?
Dr. Gül Dölen: Well, I would say&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:11.880 --> 01:53:16.560
that this is venturing off into politics, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:17.400 --> 01:53:25.740
a hobby, not my area of expertise. But I would&nbsp;
certainly say that if we look at the percentage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:26.340 --> 01:53:35.340
taxpayer money that — well, first of all, in the&nbsp;
'50s and '60s, we collected a lot more taxes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:35.340 --> 01:53:40.860
Today, we give away more in tax breaks than&nbsp;
the entire discretionary budget back to big&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:40.860 --> 01:53:45.420
corporations as incentives and tax breaks.
But really, most of those corporations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:46.440 --> 01:53:52.200
the thing that they are making and selling right&nbsp;
now, especially in the tech industry, really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:53.760 --> 01:54:01.260
are possible because the taxpayers in the&nbsp;
'50s and '60s made those investments in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:01.920 --> 01:54:13.860
the science and technology areas to develop all of&nbsp;
the computing and software building, science, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:13.860 --> 01:54:23.700
research that that stuff was built upon. Facebook&nbsp;
and all of these tech ventures that we — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:23.700 --> 01:54:30.240
Google and cell phones and computers, they didn't&nbsp;
just happen to arise out of America for no reason.&nbsp;

01:54:30.240 --> 01:54:37.260
They came because the United States for years&nbsp;
understood that this was an investment for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:37.260 --> 01:54:45.060
the way way future. So I think changing our tax&nbsp;
structure to reinvest in the future of science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:46.020 --> 01:54:51.780
it's either going to happen or it's not. It's&nbsp;
going to move to other countries and places like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:51.780 --> 01:55:00.000
China that are investing in science and technology&nbsp;
in this non-goal directed, “just throw money at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:00.000 --> 01:55:06.780
it and eventually something cool will pop out”&nbsp;
attitude. I mean, that's just my two cents. I hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:06.780 --> 01:55:10.380
I didn't insult half your audience, but —
Tim Ferriss: No.&nbsp;

01:55:10.380 --> 01:55:13.200
Dr. Gül Dölen: — that's what I think.
Tim Ferriss: I don't think you've insulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:13.200 --> 01:55:20.400
half the audience. I mean, I do think it's&nbsp;
important to say that, I mean, the priorities of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:22.440 --> 01:55:30.960
the government and nation state affect so many&nbsp;
different components of life and the future of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:32.280 --> 01:55:39.660
innovation in this country. If you do look back,&nbsp;
as you mentioned, to many of the innovations that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:39.660 --> 01:55:46.800
came out of, say, DARPA and other organizations&nbsp;
that were fundamentally funded by taxpayer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:46.800 --> 01:55:52.920
dollars, I mean, it is remarkable to look back&nbsp;
at how fundamental many of these technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:52.920 --> 01:56:00.120
have become. Switching gears and just ask a few&nbsp;
more questions. Do you have any books that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:00.120 --> 01:56:06.240
have shared, recommended, or gifted the most&nbsp;
to other people or recommended often to other&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:06.240 --> 01:56:11.400
people? Any favorite books that come to mind?
Dr. Gül Dölen: Oh, God. How did we wander into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:11.400 --> 01:56:18.960
this political sphere? Probably the ones that I&nbsp;
have gifted the most is a book called Invisible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:18.960 --> 01:56:26.640
Women by Caroline Criado Perez. I really love&nbsp;
it because I think it's a book of feminism that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:26.640 --> 01:56:36.240
is accessible and I think resonates with people&nbsp;
whether or not you're a man or a woman. It's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:36.240 --> 01:56:43.260
data. It's like, "All right, well, here's how much&nbsp;
it costs to keep ignoring women. If you fix it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:43.260 --> 01:56:48.660
your city can save a million dollars a year."
It's all about the structural stuff. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:48.660 --> 01:56:55.080
about your feelings about whether women deserve&nbsp;
to be in the kitchen or not. It's not about that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:55.080 --> 01:57:04.080
